registerClass12ExerciseBanks("phys", {"12":{"items":{"mcqs":[{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",1]],"s":["Possible combinations of three equal resistors R: all in series 3R; all in parallel R/3; two in parallel then series with the third R/2+R=3R/2; two in series then parallel with the third (2R·R)/(3R)=2R/3.","Option B is 2R/4 = R/2, which none of these combinations gives. So B cannot be obtained."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",2],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",3],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",4],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",5]],"c":1},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",6]],"s":["In parallel: x = r/n, so r = nx.","In series: R = nr = n(nx) = n²x."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",7],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",8],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",9],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",10]],"c":3},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",11]],"s":["R = V²/P: R₁ = 250²/200 = 312.5 Ω, R₂ = 250²/100 = 625 Ω.","In series: R = 937.5 Ω, so P = V²/R = 250²/937.5 = 66.7 W ≈ 67 W."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",12],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",13],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",14],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",15]],"c":1},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",16]],"s":["Stretching keeps the volume constant: length doubles, so area halves.","R′ = ρ(2L)/(A/2) = 4ρL/A = 4r."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",17],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",18],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",19],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",20]],"c":1},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",21]],"s":["I = q/t = Ne/t = (10⁶)(1.6×10⁻¹⁹ C)/(1 s).","I = 1.6×10⁻¹³ A."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",22],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",23],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",24],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",25]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",26]],"s":["Radius r → r/2, so area becomes A/4; constant volume makes length 4L.","R′ = ρ(4L)/(A/4) = 16ρL/A = 16R."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",27],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",28],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",29],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",30]],"c":0},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",31]],"s":["V = IR = (2 A)(8 Ω) = 16 A·Ω.","Since 1 V = 1 A·Ω, option C (16 A Ω) is the required 16 V."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",32],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",33],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",34],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",35]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",36]],"s":["Resistivity depends only on the material and temperature, not on length or area.","Cutting the wire halves its resistance, but the resistivity of each part remains the same."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",37],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",38],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",39],["phys12-unit12-mcq1-8-83",40]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",0]],"s":["Same length L and area A: R = ρ₁L/A + ρ₂L/A = (ρ₁+ρ₂)L/A.","The combination has length 2L and area A, so R = ρ_eq(2L)/A; therefore ρ_eq = (ρ₁+ρ₂)/2."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",1],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",2],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",3],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",4]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",5]],"s":["R = V²/P at the same voltage: R₁₀₀ = 220²/100 = 484 Ω, R₂₀₀ = 220²/200 = 242 Ω.","R₁₀₀ : R₂₀₀ = 484 : 242 = 2 : 1."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",6],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",7],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",8],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",9]],"c":1},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",10]],"s":["A thermocouple is two different metals joined at two junctions kept at different temperatures.","It converts heat energy directly into electrical energy (thermo-e.m.f., Seebeck effect)."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",11],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",12],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",13],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",14]],"c":0},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",15]],"s":["A longer wire gives a smaller potential gradient (volts per metre).","Balance lengths are then larger and can be read more precisely, so the wire should be as long as possible."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",16],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",17],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",18],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",19]],"c":0},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",20]],"s":["At the null (balance) point the galvanometer shows no current.","So no current is drawn from the unknown source and the power consumed in its circuit is ideally zero."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",21],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",22],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",23],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",24]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",25]],"s":["A Wheatstone bridge is balanced when the two midpoints are at the same potential.","Then the galvanometer current is 0 A."],"o":[["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",26],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",27],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",28],["phys12-unit12-mcq9-14-84",29]],"c":1}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",1]],"s":["Q = It = (3 A)(24 × 3600 s)","Q = 2.59 × 10⁵ C (259 200 C)."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",2]],"s":["The internal resistance of a good source is usually very small compared with the external resistance of the circuit.","The voltage drop Ir inside the source is then negligible, so the source is treated as ideal (terminal p.d. ≈ e.m.f.) to simplify the analysis."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",3]],"s":["R = ρL/A, so for equal R and L the area is A = ρL/R, i.e. A ∝ ρ.","Aluminium has a larger resistivity (≈2.8×10⁻⁸ Ω m) than copper (≈1.7×10⁻⁸ Ω m).","So the aluminium wire must have the larger cross-section and hence the larger diameter."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",4]],"s":["Terminal p.d. V = ε − Ir while the battery supplies current.","When the battery is being charged, current is forced into its positive terminal, so V = ε + Ir.","Thus the terminal p.d. exceeds the e.m.f. while the battery is being charged."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",5]],"s":["E.m.f. is the energy converted into electrical energy per unit charge by a source (chemical, mechanical, etc.) as charge passes through it.","P.d. is the electrical energy converted into other forms per unit charge between two points of a circuit (e.g. across a resistor).","E.m.f. is a property of the source and exists even with no current; p.d. exists only between two points carrying current or charge."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",6]],"s":["Junction rule (Kirchhoff's first rule): the sum of currents entering a junction equals the sum leaving; it follows from conservation of charge, because charge cannot pile up at a junction.","Loop rule (Kirchhoff's second rule): the algebraic sum of e.m.f.s and potential drops round any closed loop is zero; it follows from conservation of energy, because a charge returning to its starting point has the same potential energy.","So only the loop rule is based on conservation of energy; the junction rule is based on conservation of charge."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",7]],"s":["Rise in temperature increases the amplitude of vibration of the atoms (ions) of the conductor.","Free electrons then collide with them more often, so the drift is hindered more and resistivity rises.","Hence R = R₀[1 + α(T − T₀)] increases with temperature for metals."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",8]],"s":["No. The direction of the e.m.f. is fixed by the polarity of the battery: inside the battery it acts from the negative to the positive terminal.","The current may flow either way through the battery (discharging or charging), but the e.m.f. direction does not change."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",9]],"s":["Voltage (potential difference) is the work done per unit charge in moving a charge from one point to another.","Potential at a single point has meaning only relative to a chosen reference (e.g. earth), so a voltmeter always measures between two points."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",10]],"s":["Every e.m.f. is measured in volts and appears as a potential difference across the open terminals of the source, but it is not the same thing: e.m.f. is energy converted into electrical form, while p.d. is electrical energy converted into other forms.","A potential difference is not always an e.m.f.: the p.d. across a resistor is not an e.m.f. because no energy is converted into electrical energy there."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",11]],"s":["Q = It = (0.0001 A)(60 s)","Q = 6 × 10⁻³ C each minute."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-conceptual1-12-84-85",12]],"s":["At balance P/Q = R/S, so the two points joined by the galvanometer are at the same potential.","With no potential difference across the galvanometer, no current flows through it."]}],"longQuestions":[{"q":[["phys12-unit12-comprehensive1-7-85",1]],"s":["Electric current is the rate of flow of charge: I = ΔQ/Δt. A steady current is one whose value does not change with time.","Current can be due to positive charges (e.g. positive ions in electrolytes and gases), negative charges (electrons in metals) or both (electrolytes, ionised gases).","A flow of negative charge in one direction is equivalent to a flow of positive charge in the opposite direction, so conventional current is taken in the direction of positive-charge flow.","Unit: the ampere. One ampere is the current when one coulomb of charge passes a cross-section in one second (1 A = 1 C s⁻¹)."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-comprehensive1-7-85",2]],"s":["A metal contains a large number n of free electrons per unit volume moving randomly at high speed; with no field their net flow is zero.","When a p.d. is applied, the electric field E exerts a force −eE, so the electrons acquire a small average drift velocity v_d opposite to E, superimposed on their random motion.","In time t, electrons in length v_d t pass a cross-section A: charge Q = nAv_d t e, so I = Q/t = nAev_d.","Because n is very large, a tiny drift velocity (about 10⁻⁴ m s⁻¹) gives an appreciable current; the current direction is opposite to the electron drift."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-comprehensive1-7-85",3]],"s":["Ohm's law: the current through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across it, provided its temperature and other physical conditions remain constant: V = IR.","Scope and validity: it holds for metallic conductors at constant temperature (ohmic conductors, straight-line V–I graph). It does not hold for diodes, transistors, filament lamps whose temperature changes, gas discharge tubes, etc.","(a) Resistance R = ρL/A, where ρ is the resistivity (Ω m), a property of the material. Conductivity σ = 1/ρ (Ω⁻¹ m⁻¹ or S m⁻¹); good conductors have small ρ and large σ.","(b) For metals, resistance increases nearly linearly with temperature: R_t = R₀[1 + α(t − t₀)], where α is the temperature coefficient of resistance. Semiconductors and carbon have negative α: their resistance falls as temperature rises."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-comprehensive1-7-85",4]],"s":["E.m.f. (ε): the energy converted into electrical energy per unit charge by the source; it equals the terminal p.d. when no current is drawn. Unit: volt.","Internal resistance (r): the resistance offered by the material of the source (electrolyte, electrodes) to the current flowing through it.","Terminal potential difference (V): the p.d. between the terminals when the source delivers current I. Since part of the e.m.f. is used inside the source, V = ε − Ir.","When no current flows, V = ε; when charging, V = ε + Ir."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-comprehensive1-7-85",5]],"s":["Construction: a rheostat is a long resistance wire (e.g. nichrome) wound on an insulating cylinder, with two fixed end terminals A, B and a sliding contact C moving on a metal rod along the coil.","(a) As a current controller: connect one end terminal A and the sliding terminal C in series with the circuit. Moving C changes the length of wire in the circuit and hence the resistance, so the current is controlled.","(b) As a potential divider: connect the fixed ends A and B across the supply V. The output is taken between A and the slider C. Since the same current flows through the whole wire, V_out = V × (R_AC/R_AB), which can be varied from 0 to V by moving C."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-comprehensive1-7-85",6]],"s":["A Wheatstone bridge is a circuit of four resistances P, Q, R and S joined in a closed loop ABCD, with a cell between A and C and a galvanometer between B and D.","Principle and balance: the resistances are adjusted until the galvanometer shows no deflection. Then points B and D are at the same potential.","With no galvanometer current, I₁ flows through P and Q, I₂ through R and S. V_AB = V_AD gives I₁P = I₂R, and V_BC = V_DC gives I₁Q = I₂S. Dividing: P/Q = R/S.","Working: if three resistances are known, the unknown S = QR/P. The balance condition does not depend on the cell's e.m.f., so the method is accurate."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-comprehensive1-7-85",7]],"s":["A potentiometer is a device for measuring and comparing potential differences/e.m.f.s accurately without drawing current from the source being measured.","Principle: when a steady current flows through a uniform wire, the potential drop across a length l is proportional to l (V = kl, k = potential gradient).","Construction: a long uniform wire AB (often 1–10 m) stretched on a board with a metre scale; a driver cell and rheostat send a steady current through it; the cell under test is joined (same polarity) to A and through a galvanometer to a sliding jockey.","Working: the jockey is moved until the galvanometer shows no deflection at balance length l. Then ε = kl. Comparing two cells: ε₁/ε₂ = l₁/l₂.","Advantage: at balance no current is drawn from the cell under test, so its full e.m.f. (not the terminal p.d.) is measured; it is a null method and is more accurate than a voltmeter."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-comprehensive8-9-86",1]],"s":["A thermocouple is a pair of different metals (e.g. copper and iron) joined at two junctions. When the junctions are kept at different temperatures an e.m.f. is produced and current flows — the Seebeck effect.","Working: keep the cold junction at 0 °C (in ice) and heat the hot junction; a galvanometer in the circuit shows a current that increases with the temperature difference.","Variation of e.m.f.: the graph of thermo-e.m.f. against the hot-junction temperature is a parabola. The e.m.f. rises to a maximum at the neutral temperature θₙ, then falls, becoming zero at the temperature of inversion θᵢ and reversing beyond it.","θₙ is fixed for a given pair of metals; θᵢ − θₙ = θₙ − θ_c (θ_c = cold-junction temperature). Thermocouples are used as thermometers and as sources of small e.m.f."]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-comprehensive8-9-86",2]],"s":["Complex circuits with several sources and loops cannot be reduced to simple series/parallel combinations, so Kirchhoff's rules are used to find all currents.","Rule 1 (junction rule): ΣI entering = ΣI leaving at any junction (conservation of charge).","Rule 2 (loop rule): around any closed loop Σε = ΣIR, i.e. the algebraic sum of potential changes is zero (conservation of energy). Sign convention: a resistor traversed in the current direction gives −IR; a cell traversed from − to + gives +ε.","Example: branch 1 has ε₁ = 12 V with R₁ = 2 Ω, branch 2 has ε₂ = 9 V with R₂ = 1 Ω, and both feed a common branch R₃ = 2 Ω. Junction: I₃ = I₁ + I₂. Loop 1: 12 = 2I₁ + 2(I₁ + I₂), i.e. 4I₁ + 2I₂ = 12. Loop 2: 9 = 1·I₂ + 2(I₁ + I₂), i.e. 2I₁ + 3I₂ = 9.","Solving: I₁ = 2.25 A, I₂ = 1.5 A, I₃ = 3.75 A. Check loop 1: 2(2.25) + 2(3.75) = 12 V ✓.","The rules are applied to as many independent junctions and loops as there are unknown currents."]}],"numericals":[{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",1]],"s":["R = ε/I = 12.8 V / 3.2 A = 4 Ω.","Q = It = (3.2 A)(5 × 60 s) = 960 C."],"a":"R = 4 Ω; Q = 960 C","n":"The book prints 1200 C. With the stated 3.2 A for 5 minutes the charge is 960 C (1200 C would need 6.25 minutes).","p":["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",2]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",3]],"s":["R = V²/P.","40 W bulb: R = 120²/40 = 360 Ω. 60 W bulb: R = 120²/60 = 240 Ω.","The 40 W bulb has the greater filament resistance, because at the same voltage a smaller power means a smaller current, P = V²/R."],"a":"40 W bulb: 360 Ω (greater); 60 W bulb: 240 Ω","p":["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",4]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",5]],"s":["R = R₀[1 + α(t − t₀)] = 0.125[1 + (−0.0005)(85 − 20)]","R = 0.125 × 0.9675 = 0.121 Ω"],"a":"R ≈ 0.12 Ω","p":["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",6]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",7]],"s":["A = πr² = π(1 × 10⁻³ m)² = 3.14 × 10⁻⁶ m².","R = ρL/A = (2.63 × 10⁻⁸ Ω m)(10 m)/(3.14 × 10⁻⁶ m²)","R = 0.0837 Ω"],"a":"R ≈ 0.084 Ω","p":["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",8]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",9]],"s":["Terminal voltage: V = ε − Ir = 12 − (100)(0.012) = 10.8 V.","Load resistance: R = V/I = 10.8/100 = 0.108 Ω.","Power of the source: P_ε = εI = 12 × 100 = 1200 W.","Power in the load: P_R = I²R = 100² × 0.108 = 1080 W; power lost inside: P_r = I²r = 100² × 0.012 = 120 W (check: 1080 + 120 = 1200 W)."],"a":"V = 10.8 V, R = 0.108 Ω, P_ε = 1200 W, P_R = 1080 W, P_r = 120 W","n":"The printed answer omits P_R; it is 1080 W.","p":["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",10]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",11]],"s":["P = I²R, so I = √(P/R) = √(10/120)","I = 0.289 A"],"a":"I ≈ 0.289 A","p":["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",12]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",13]],"s":["(a) P = V²/R = 100²/50 = 200 W.","(b) E = Pt = 200 × 3600 = 7.2 × 10⁵ J = 0.72 MJ."],"a":"(a) 200 W (b) 0.72 MJ","p":["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",14]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",15]],"s":["I = ε/(R + r) = 120/(1000 + 0.01)","I = 0.120 A = 120 mA"],"a":"I ≈ 120 mA","p":["phys12-unit12-numericals1-8-86",16]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numerical9-87",1]],"s":["From the figure: a 24 V cell (r = 0.1 Ω) and a 6 V cell (r = 0.9 Ω) in opposition with an 8 Ω resistor in one loop.","I = (24 − 6)/(8 + 0.1 + 0.9) = 18/9 = 2 A.","24 V cell (discharging): V = ε − Ir = 24 − 2(0.1) = 23.8 V.","6 V cell (being charged): V = ε + Ir = 6 + 2(0.9) = 7.8 V."],"a":"23.8 V and 7.8 V","n":"Values read from the printed figure (shown above); they reproduce the printed answers.","d":"section:phys12-unit12-numerical9-87","p":["phys12-unit12-numerical9-87",2]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numerical10-87",1]],"s":["The ideal voltmeter draws no current; R₁ and R₂ are in parallel across the source: R_p = (100 × 300)/(100 + 300) = 75 Ω.","I = ε/(R_p + r) = 15/(75 + 5) = 0.1875 A.","Terminal voltage V = IR_p = 0.1875 × 75 = 14.06 V.","Current in R₁: I₁ = V/R₁ = 14.06/100 = 0.1406 A."],"a":"I₁ ≈ 0.141 A","n":"The book prints 0.01406 A; with the stated values the current is 0.1406 A (the printed decimal point is misplaced).","d":"section:phys12-unit12-numerical10-87","p":["phys12-unit12-numerical10-87",2]},{"q":[["phys12-unit12-numerical11-87",1]],"s":["At balance P/Q = R/S.","With P = Q = R = 75 Ω: S = QR/P = (75 × 75)/75 = 75 Ω."],"a":"75 Ω","p":["phys12-unit12-numerical11-87",2]}]}},"13":{"items":{"mcqs":[{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",1]],"s":["F = I L × B. Take east = x̂, north = ŷ, up = ẑ: L is along ŷ and B along −x̂.","ŷ × (−x̂) = +ẑ, so the force is upward."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",2]],"s":["Each wire lies in the magnetic field of the other; with parallel currents the forces F = μ₀I₁I₂L/(2πd) pull the wires together.","Currents in the same direction attract."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",3]],"s":["1 Wb = 1 T m², and 1 T = 1 N/(A m).","So 1 Wb = 1 N m²/(A m) = 1 N m/A."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",4]],"s":["Φ = BA cos θ, where θ is the angle between B and the vector area.","Φ is maximum (= BA) when θ = 0°."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",5]],"s":["Ampere's law ∮B·dl = μ₀I is a line integral taken round a closed path (Amperian loop)."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",6]],"s":["L = μ₀N²A/l for a solenoid.","Doubling N with the same length multiplies L by 2² = 4."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",7]],"s":["F = qvB sin θ; a charge at rest has v = 0.","So the magnetic force is zero."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",8]],"s":["The magnetic force qv × B is always perpendicular to the velocity.","No component of force acts along the displacement, so the work done is zero (speed stays constant)."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",9]],"s":["A compass north pole points towards the Earth's geographic north.","Unlike poles attract, so the Earth's northern magnetic pole behaves like the south pole of a magnet."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",10]],"s":["Concave pole pieces with a soft-iron cylinder inside the coil make the field radial.","In a radial field the plane of the coil is always parallel to B, so the torque NIAB is the same at every position and the deflection is proportional to the current."],"n":"The soft-iron core also concentrates (strengthens) the field, so some keys give D; the purpose of making the pole pieces concave is to make the field radial.","inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",11]],"s":["A small resistance (shunt) in parallel lets most of the current bypass the galvanometer.","The combination has low resistance and measures large currents: it is an ammeter."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",12]],"s":["A voltmeter measures the potential difference between two points, so it is connected across (in parallel with) the component."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",13]],"s":["An ideal voltmeter draws no current from the circuit, so its resistance is infinite."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-multiple-choice-questions1-14-122-123",14]],"s":["τ = NIAB cos α.","Increasing the number of turns, the current, the magnetic field or the area of the coil all increase the torque."],"inline":1,"c":3}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["phys13-exercise-conceptual-questions1-9-123",1]],"s":["The charged plastic rod is at rest, so it produces only an electric field, not a magnetic field.","The deflection is electrostatic: the rod's field polarises (induces charges on) the needle and attracts it. It is not a magnetic effect."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-conceptual-questions1-9-123",2]],"s":["Permeability μ measures how easily a medium supports magnetic field lines (B = μH); unit H m⁻¹. For free space μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ H m⁻¹.","Permittivity ε measures how a medium affects (reduces) the electric field and electric force between charges, F = q₁q₂/(4πεr²); unit F m⁻¹ (C² N⁻¹ m⁻²). For free space ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² F m⁻¹.","Permeability belongs to magnetism; permittivity belongs to electrostatics."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-conceptual-questions1-9-123",3]],"s":["Yes — a rotating object produces a magnetic field if it carries charge, because moving charges form a current.","Examples: a charged disc or sphere spinning about its axis; the rotation of the Earth's molten iron core produces the Earth's magnetic field; spinning and orbiting electrons make atoms magnetic."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-conceptual-questions1-9-123",4]],"s":["Both have the same charge magnitude e and speed v, so the force F = evB is the same.","Radius r = mv/(eB): the electron's mass is about 1/1836 of the proton's, so its path has a much smaller radius.","Hence the electron suffers the greater deflection (it also curves the opposite way)."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-conceptual-questions1-9-123",5]],"s":["Yes. If the particle moves parallel or anti-parallel to B, F = qvB sin θ = 0 and it continues in a straight line.","It can also go straight in a non-zero B if an electric force qE exactly balances qvB (crossed fields, velocity selector)."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-conceptual-questions1-9-123",6]],"s":["Adjacent turns of the spring carry current in the same direction.","Parallel currents in the same direction attract, so the turns pull together and the spring contracts."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-conceptual-questions1-9-123",7]],"s":["They cannot. Neutrons have no charge, so the electric field between the dees cannot accelerate them and the magnetic field cannot bend them.","A cyclotron accelerates only charged particles (protons, deuterons, α-particles); fast neutrons are produced indirectly, e.g. by striking a target with accelerated deuterons."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-conceptual-questions1-9-123",8]],"s":["The loop turns until its plane is perpendicular to B, i.e. its magnetic moment m = NIA is parallel to B.","In this position the torque τ = m × B is zero and the equilibrium is stable (potential energy −mB is minimum)."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-conceptual-questions1-9-123",9]],"s":["A current-carrying coil produces a magnetic field like that of a bar magnet: lines emerge from one face and enter the other.","The face where the current appears anticlockwise acts as a north pole and the clockwise face as a south pole.","Like a bar magnet it has a magnetic moment m = NIA, aligns itself with an external field, and attracts or repels other magnets."]}],"longQuestions":[{"q":[["phys13-exercise-comprehensive-questions1-7-124",1]],"s":["Consider a straight conductor of length L and cross-section A placed in a uniform field B at angle θ to the current. It contains n free charge carriers per unit volume, each of charge q moving with drift velocity v_d.","Force on one carrier: F₁ = qv_dB sin θ. Number of carriers in the conductor: N = nAL.","Total force: F = nAL q v_d B sin θ. Since the current is I = nAqv_d, F = ILB sin θ.","In vector form F = I L × B, where L points along the current. The force is perpendicular to both L and B (right-hand rule / Fleming's left-hand rule).","F is maximum (ILB) when the conductor is perpendicular to B and zero when it is parallel to B."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-comprehensive-questions1-7-124",2]],"s":["Take a rectangular coil of N turns, sides a (along the axis of rotation) and b, carrying current I in a uniform field B; let α be the angle between the plane of the coil and B.","The two sides of length b feel equal and opposite forces along the axis that cancel. The two sides of length a feel forces F = NIaB, equal, opposite and not in line — they form a couple.","Perpendicular distance between the lines of action of the couple: b cos α.","Torque: τ = F × b cos α = NIaB b cos α = NIAB cos α, where A = ab is the area.","If θ is the angle between the normal to the coil and B (θ = 90° − α), τ = NIAB sin θ, or τ = m × B with magnetic moment m = NIA. τ is maximum when the plane of the coil is parallel to B."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-comprehensive-questions1-7-124",3]],"s":["Yes. A charge q moving with velocity v in a magnetic field B experiences the magnetic (Lorentz) force F = q v × B, of magnitude F = qvB sin θ.","The force is perpendicular to both v and B; its direction is given by the right-hand rule (reversed for a negative charge).","F = 0 if the charge is at rest or moves parallel to B; F is maximum (qvB) when v ⊥ B.","Because F ⊥ v, the force does no work: it changes the direction but not the speed. A charge moving perpendicular to a uniform B moves in a circle of radius r = mv/(qB).","With an electric field also present, the total Lorentz force is F = qE + q v × B."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-comprehensive-questions1-7-124",4]],"s":["Electrons from a heated filament are accelerated through a potential difference V: ½mv² = eV, so v = √(2eV/m).","The beam enters a uniform magnetic field B (produced by Helmholtz coils) at right angles, so the magnetic force provides the centripetal force: evB = mv²/r, giving v = eBr/m.","Equating the two expressions for v: e²B²r²/m² = 2eV/m, so e/m = 2V/(B²r²).","The beam moves in a circle made visible by the gas in a fine-beam tube; V, B and the radius r are measured.","The accepted value is e/m = 1.76 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-comprehensive-questions1-7-124",5]],"s":["Magnetic flux Φ through a surface is the number of magnetic field lines passing through it; it is the scalar product of B and the vector area A: Φ = B · A = BA cos θ.","θ is the angle between B and the normal (vector area) of the surface.","Φ is maximum (BA) when the field is perpendicular to the surface (θ = 0°) and zero when it is parallel to the surface (θ = 90°).","SI unit: weber (Wb), 1 Wb = 1 T m² = 1 N m A⁻¹. Magnetic flux density B = Φ/A⊥, measured in tesla (Wb m⁻²).","For a non-uniform field or curved surface, Φ = ∫ B · dA."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-comprehensive-questions1-7-124",6]],"s":["Ampere's law: the line integral of B round any closed path equals μ₀ times the total current enclosed by the path: ∮ B · dl = μ₀I.","Solenoid: a long coil with n turns per unit length carrying current I. The field inside is uniform and parallel to the axis; outside it is nearly zero.","Choose a rectangular Amperian loop abcd: side ab of length l inside along the axis, cd outside, bc and da perpendicular to the axis.","Contributions: along ab, B l; along cd, 0 (B ≈ 0 outside); along bc and da, 0 (B ⊥ dl inside, B = 0 outside). So ∮ B · dl = Bl.","Current enclosed = (number of turns nl) × I. Hence Bl = μ₀nlI, so B = μ₀nI (or B = μ₀NI/L)."]},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-comprehensive-questions1-7-124",7]],"s":["A galvanometer is a sensitive instrument for detecting and measuring small currents. A coil pivoted in a radial magnetic field turns until the deflecting torque NIAB balances the restoring torque cθ of the spring, so θ = (NAB/c) I ∝ I.","Ammeter: connect a small resistance (shunt) R_s in parallel. If the galvanometer (resistance R_g) gives full-scale deflection at I_g and the ammeter range is I, then I_gR_g = (I − I_g)R_s, so R_s = I_gR_g/(I − I_g). The ammeter has very low resistance and is joined in series in a circuit.","Voltmeter: connect a high resistance R_h in series. For range V: V = I_g(R_g + R_h), so R_h = V/I_g − R_g. The voltmeter has very high resistance and is joined in parallel across the component."]}],"numericals":[{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",1]],"s":["B = μ₀I/(2πr), so r = μ₀I/(2πB).","r = (4π × 10⁻⁷ × 10)/(2π × 5 × 10⁻⁵) = (2 × 10⁻⁶)/(5 × 10⁻⁵)","r = 0.04 m"],"a":"0.04 m"},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",2]],"s":["n = N/l = 1000/0.5 = 2000 turns m⁻¹.","B = μ₀nI, so I = B/(μ₀n) = 2.5 × 10⁻³/(4π × 10⁻⁷ × 2000)","I = 0.99 A ≈ 1 A"],"a":"≈ 1 A"},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",3]],"s":["F = qvB (v ⊥ B), so v = F/(qB).","v = (2 × 10⁻¹²)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.1)","v = 1.25 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ ≈ 1.3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹"],"a":"1.25 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹"},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",4]],"s":["K.E. = 8 MeV = 8 × 10⁶ × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 1.28 × 10⁻¹² J.","v = √(2K/m) = √(2 × 1.28 × 10⁻¹²/1.67 × 10⁻²⁷) = 3.92 × 10⁷ m s⁻¹.","(a) F = qvB = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 3.92 × 10⁷ × 2.5 = 1.57 × 10⁻¹¹ N ≈ 1.6 × 10⁻¹¹ N.","(b) r = mv/(qB) = (1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ × 3.92 × 10⁷)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 2.5) = 0.16 m."],"a":"(a) 1.6 × 10⁻¹¹ N (b) ≈ 0.16 m","n":"Part (a) says 'force on the electron'; the particle is the proton. The radius works out to 0.16 m (the book rounds to 0.17 m)."},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",5]],"s":["T = 2πm/(qB); the radius is not needed because T does not depend on speed.","T = 2π × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 10 × 10⁻³)","T = 3.6 × 10⁻⁹ s = 3.6 ns"],"a":"3.6 ns"},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",6]],"s":["A = 0.06 × 0.04 = 2.4 × 10⁻³ m².","τ_max = NIAB, so I = τ/(NAB) = 0.20/(250 × 2.4 × 10⁻³ × 0.25)","I = 0.20/0.15 = 1.33 A"],"a":"I ≈ 1.3 A","n":"The book prints 1.5 A; the stated data give 1.33 A."},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",7]],"s":["Zero net force: eE = evB, so v = E/B.","v = 2.00 × 10⁵/0.105","v = 1.9 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹"],"a":"1.9 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹"},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",8]],"s":["Shunt: R_s = I_gR_g/(I − I_g).","R_s = (0.01 × 100)/(100 − 0.01) = 1/99.99","R_s ≈ 0.01 Ω, connected in parallel with the galvanometer."],"a":"R_s ≈ 0.01 Ω in parallel"},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",9]],"s":["Series resistance: R_h = V/I_g − R_g.","R_h = 20/(5 × 10⁻³) − 100 = 4000 − 100","R_h = 3900 Ω, connected in series with the galvanometer."],"a":"R_h = 3900 Ω in series","n":"The book prints 1900 Ω, which would give a 10 V range; for 20 V the series resistance is 3900 Ω."},{"q":[["phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",10]],"s":["At the midpoint each wire is r = 0.05 m away. With opposite currents the two fields point the same way and add.","Each: B = μ₀I/(2πr) = (2 × 10⁻⁷ × 8)/0.05 = 3.2 × 10⁻⁵ T.","Total B = 2 × 3.2 × 10⁻⁵ = 6.4 × 10⁻⁵ T"],"a":"6.4 × 10⁻⁵ T"}]}},"14":{"items":{"mcqs":[{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",1]],"s":["Faraday's law: ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt. An e.m.f. is induced whenever the flux linking the coil changes.","A closed loop is needed only for an induced current to flow, not for the e.m.f. itself. So the basic requirement for inducing an e.m.f. is a change in flux (B)."],"n":"Some keys give D because a closed circuit is needed for an induced current; for an induced e.m.f. only option B is required.","inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",2]],"s":["ε = NBAω sin ωt changes direction every half revolution.","So the induced e.m.f. is alternating."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",3]],"s":["A dynamically induced e.m.f. is produced when a conductor moves through a magnetic field.","This is the principle of a generator (a transformer and a choke use statically induced e.m.f.)."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",4]],"s":["Fleming's left-hand rule (motor rule): First finger — Field, seCond finger — Current, thuMb — Motion (force).","The thumb gives the direction of the mechanical force."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",5]],"s":["By symmetry the perpendicular components of the fields of opposite elements of the coil cancel.","The field on the axis of a current-carrying coil is parallel to the axis."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",6]],"s":["ε = NBAω sin ωt, so the induced current I = ε/R varies continuously (sinusoidally) with time."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",7]],"s":["Peak e.m.f. ε₀ = NBAω is proportional to N.","Increasing the number of turns increases the e.m.f."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",8]],"s":["A statically induced e.m.f. arises when the flux changes while the conductor is stationary.","In a transformer the coils do not move; the changing primary current changes the flux. The other devices are generators (dynamically induced e.m.f.)."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",9]],"s":["M = Δ(NΦ)/ΔI = (2 × 10⁻² Wb)/(0.01 A).","M = 2 H."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",10]],"s":["Faraday's law: ε = −N dΦ/dt.","The induced e.m.f. is proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux through the coil."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",11]],"s":["ε = L ΔI/Δt, so L = εΔt/ΔI = (8 × 0.05)/(4 − 2).","L = 0.4/2 = 0.2 H."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",12]],"s":["An ideal transformer only changes voltage and current: V_pI_p = V_sI_s.","Power remains constant (a step-up transformer raises voltage and lowers current)."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",13]],"s":["V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p = 3/2 for a step-up transformer.","V_s = 30 × 3/2 = 45 V."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",14]],"s":["Eddy currents are induced in a bulk metal when the magnetic flux through it changes.","So they are produced when a metal is kept in a varying magnetic field (or moves through a field)."],"inline":1,"c":0}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",16]],"s":["Back e.m.f. ε_b = NBAω sin ωt (average ∝ NBAω): it depends on the number of turns, the magnetic field, the area of the armature coil and the speed of rotation.","It is always less than the applied voltage V, since I = (V − ε_b)/R must be positive for the motor to run; so it is limited by the supply voltage and the load (speed)."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",17]],"s":["When the load (rate of doing work) increases, the motor needs more torque and slows down.","Back e.m.f. is proportional to the speed (ω), so it decreases.","The current I = (V − ε_b)/R then increases, giving the larger torque and power needed."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",18]],"s":["Induced e.m.f. ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt is proportional to the number of turns N.","Coils with many turns give a larger e.m.f. and current for the same flux change, so the galvanometer deflection is larger and easier to observe."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",19]],"s":["Unit of flux: 1 Wb = 1 T m² = 1 (N A⁻¹ m⁻¹) m² = 1 N m A⁻¹ = 1 J A⁻¹.","So ΔΦ/Δt has unit J A⁻¹ s⁻¹ = J C⁻¹ = V, the unit of e.m.f.","Dimensions: [Φ] = [ML²T⁻²A⁻¹], so [Φ/t] = [ML²T⁻³A⁻¹] = [ε]. The equation is dimensionally correct (the minus sign is dimensionless)."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",20]],"s":["Flux linkage = NΦ = NBA cos θ, where θ is the angle between B and the normal to the coil.","For a coil rotating with angular velocity ω, θ = ωt: NΦ = NBA cos ωt, which is maximum (NBA) when the plane of the coil is perpendicular to B and zero when it is parallel."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",21]],"s":["A metal disc or plate attached to the moving wheel or rail passes between the poles of a strong electromagnet.","The changing flux induces eddy currents in the metal; by Lenz's law their magnetic field opposes the motion, producing a braking force.","Kinetic energy is changed into heat. The braking is smooth, frictionless and stronger at higher speed (used in trains and roller coasters)."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",22]],"s":["Make the core of thin laminated sheets of soft (silicon) iron insulated from one another by varnish or oxide.","Lamination breaks the large eddy-current loops and the high-resistivity material reduces the current, so the I²R loss is small. (Ferrite cores are used at high frequency.)"]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",23]],"s":["Under the glass-ceramic top is a coil carrying high-frequency alternating current.","Its changing magnetic field induces eddy currents in the iron/steel base of the cooking pot.","The I²R heating of the eddy currents heats the pot directly, while the cooktop surface itself stays relatively cool."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",0]],"s":["When the armature of a motor rotates in the magnetic field, an e.m.f. is induced in it (generator action).","By Lenz's law this e.m.f. opposes the applied voltage and is called back e.m.f. ε_b. The armature current is I = (V − ε_b)/R; it is small at full speed and large at start-up."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",1]],"s":["In a radial field the plane of the coil is always parallel to the field lines.","The torque NIAB (and the induced e.m.f.) is then maximum and constant at all positions, giving smooth uniform rotation and a deflection proportional to current, instead of the varying torque NIAB cos α of a uniform field."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",2]],"s":["(a) Laminated soft iron.","(b) Soft iron has high permeability (concentrates flux, good flux linkage) and low hysteresis loss (easily magnetised and demagnetised). Lamination reduces eddy-current loss. Solid iron would have large eddy currents, and aluminium is non-magnetic."]}],"longQuestions":[{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",4]],"s":["Electromagnetic induction is the production of an e.m.f. (and current) in a circuit when the magnetic flux linked with it changes.","Experiment 1: a coil is connected to a galvanometer. Pushing a bar magnet into the coil deflects the galvanometer; withdrawing it gives the opposite deflection; no deflection when the magnet is at rest.","Experiment 2: a straight wire connected to a galvanometer is moved across the field between magnet poles — deflection occurs while it moves.","Experiment 3: two coils side by side; switching the current in the primary on or off induces a current in the secondary.","Magnitude depends on the rate of change of flux: speed of motion, strength of the magnet, number of turns and area of the coil (ε = −NΔΦ/Δt). Direction depends on the direction of motion/flux change and is given by Lenz's law (or Fleming's right-hand rule)."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",5]],"s":["Faraday's law: the e.m.f. induced in a circuit is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux linked with it: ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt.","Statically induced e.m.f.: the conductor is stationary and the flux changes, e.g. switching or varying the current in a primary coil induces e.m.f. in a nearby secondary coil (mutual induction, transformer) or in the coil itself (self-induction).","Dynamically induced e.m.f.: the conductor moves and cuts magnetic field lines, e.g. a rod moved across a magnetic field (ε = BLv) or a coil rotated in a field (generator).","In both cases a galvanometer shows a deflection only while the flux is changing."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",6]],"s":["Lenz's law: the direction of the induced current is always such that it opposes the cause (change of flux) producing it.","Example: when the north pole of a magnet is pushed towards a coil, the near face of the coil becomes a north pole and repels the magnet; when the magnet is withdrawn, the face becomes a south pole and attracts it.","Hence work must always be done against this opposing force to move the magnet. This mechanical work is converted into the electrical energy of the induced current (and then into heat).","If the induced current aided the motion, the magnet would accelerate by itself and electrical energy would be produced without work — violating conservation of energy. So Lenz's law is a consequence of the law of conservation of energy."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",7]],"s":["Mutual induction: when the current in one coil (primary) changes, the changing flux linking a neighbouring coil (secondary) induces an e.m.f. in the secondary.","The flux linkage of the secondary is proportional to the primary current: N_sΦ_s ∝ I_p, so N_sΦ_s = MI_p.","Induced e.m.f.: ε_s = −N_s ΔΦ_s/Δt = −M ΔI_p/Δt.","Coefficient of mutual induction M: the e.m.f. induced in the secondary when the current in the primary changes at the rate of 1 A s⁻¹ (or flux linkage per unit primary current). It depends on the number of turns, areas, separation, orientation and the core material.","Unit: henry (H); 1 H = 1 V s A⁻¹ = 1 Wb A⁻¹."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",8]],"s":["Self-induction: when the current in a coil changes, the changing flux of its own field induces an e.m.f. in the same coil, opposing the change (Lenz).","Flux linkage is proportional to current: NΦ = LI.","Induced e.m.f.: ε = −N ΔΦ/Δt = −L ΔI/Δt.","Coefficient of self-induction L: the e.m.f. induced in a coil when the current through it changes at 1 A s⁻¹. For a solenoid L = μ₀N²A/l; it increases with turns, area and an iron core.","Unit: henry (H), 1 H = 1 V s A⁻¹. Self-inductance opposes changes of current (electrical inertia), e.g. a choke."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",9]],"s":["Motional e.m.f. is the e.m.f. induced in a conductor moving through a magnetic field.","A rod of length l slides with velocity v on two rails perpendicular to a uniform field B. Each free charge q in the rod feels a force F = qvB along the rod, so charges separate and an e.m.f. appears.","In time Δt the rod moves vΔt, so the area of the circuit changes by ΔA = lvΔt and the flux changes by ΔΦ = BΔA = Blv Δt.","Faraday's law: ε = ΔΦ/Δt = Blv. (Equivalently, work per unit charge = Fl/q = Blv.)","If the rod moves at angle θ to B, ε = Blv sin θ; its direction is given by Fleming's right-hand rule."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",10]],"s":["A generator changes mechanical energy into electrical energy by electromagnetic induction. A d.c. generator uses a split-ring commutator so the output current always flows in one direction.","A rectangular coil of N turns and area A rotates with angular velocity ω in a uniform field B. At time t the normal makes angle θ = ωt with B, so the flux linkage is NBA cos ωt.","Induced e.m.f.: ε = −d(NΦ)/dt = NBAω sin ωt = ε₀ sin ωt, with peak value ε₀ = NBAω.","ε is zero when the plane of the coil is perpendicular to B and maximum when it is parallel; it reverses every half rotation — an alternating e.m.f. Slip rings give a.c.; a commutator reverses the connections every half turn to give pulsating d.c."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",11]],"s":["An a.c. motor converts electrical energy into mechanical energy using alternating current.","Construction: a rectangular coil (armature) on an axle between the poles of a magnet (or field winding), connected to the a.c. supply through slip rings and carbon brushes (a synchronous motor) — or, in an induction motor, a stator producing a rotating magnetic field and a cage rotor.","Working: the current in the armature in the magnetic field experiences forces F = ILB on opposite sides, forming a couple that rotates it. In the induction motor the rotating field induces currents in the rotor, and by Lenz's law the rotor follows the rotating field.","Uses: fans, pumps, washing machines and other household and industrial machines."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",12]],"s":["A transformer changes an alternating voltage from one value to another without change of frequency.","Principle: mutual induction. An alternating current in the primary coil produces a changing flux in a common laminated soft-iron core, which induces an e.m.f. in the secondary coil.","Since the same flux links every turn: V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p. For an ideal transformer (no losses) V_pI_p = V_sI_s, so I_s/I_p = N_p/N_s. N_s > N_p: step-up; N_s < N_p: step-down.","A laminated core (thin insulated sheets) is used because the changing flux induces eddy currents in the core; lamination breaks up their paths and greatly reduces the heating (energy loss). Soft iron also reduces hysteresis loss."]},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",13]],"s":["When the armature coil of a motor rotates in the magnetic field, it cuts the field lines and an e.m.f. is induced in it, as in a generator.","By Lenz's law this e.m.f. opposes the applied voltage V and is called the back e.m.f. ε_b. The net voltage driving the armature current is V − ε_b, so I = (V − ε_b)/R.","At starting the speed and ε_b are zero, so a large current flows; a starting resistance is used to protect the coil.","As speed rises ε_b increases and the current falls. Under a heavy load the motor slows, ε_b falls, and more current flows to give the extra torque. The back e.m.f. therefore regulates the motor's current and power: Vᴵ = ε_bI + I²R."]}],"numericals":[{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",15]],"s":["Each spoke rotates about the axle: ε = ½BωL². The spokes are in parallel, so their number does not change the e.m.f.","ω = 180 rev/min = 3 rev/s = 6π rad s⁻¹ = 18.85 rad s⁻¹; B = 0.6 G = 0.6 × 10⁻⁴ T.","ε = ½ × 0.6 × 10⁻⁴ × 18.85 × (0.6)² = 2.04 × 10⁻⁴ V"],"a":"≈ 2.04 × 10⁻⁴ V"},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",16]],"s":["ΔΦ = ΔB × A = (1.4 − 1.0) × 20 × 10⁻⁴ = 8 × 10⁻⁴ Wb.","L = N ΔΦ/ΔI = 1000 × 8 × 10⁻⁴/(9 − 4) = 0.16 H.","ε = L ΔI/Δt = 0.16 × 5/0.05 = 16 V."],"a":"L = 0.16 H; ε = 16 V"},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",17]],"s":["ΔΦ = 1 − 0.2 = 0.8 mWb = 8 × 10⁻⁴ Wb.","ε = N ΔΦ/Δt = 100 × 8 × 10⁻⁴/0.1 = 0.8 V.","I = ε/(R_coil + R_G) = 0.8/(100 + 400) = 1.6 × 10⁻³ A = 1.6 mA."],"a":"ε = 0.8 V; I = 1.6 mA"},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",18]],"s":["(a) ε = BLv = 0.30 × 10⁻⁴ × 10 × 5.0 = 1.5 × 10⁻³ V.","(b) Force on positive charges q v × B: v is downward, the horizontal component of the Earth's field points north; down × north = east. The e.m.f. acts from west to east along the wire.","(c) Positive charge collects at the east end, so the east end is at the higher potential."],"a":"(a) 1.5 × 10⁻³ V (b) west → east (c) the east end"},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",19]],"s":["ε = L ΔI/Δt, so L = εΔt/ΔI.","L = 200 × 0.1/5.0","L = 4 H"],"a":"4 H"},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",20]],"s":["n = 15 turns/cm = 1500 turns m⁻¹. Inside the solenoid B = μ₀nI, so ΔB = μ₀nΔI = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 1500 × 2.0 = 3.77 × 10⁻³ T.","ΔΦ = ΔB × A = 3.77 × 10⁻³ × 2.0 × 10⁻⁴ = 7.54 × 10⁻⁷ Wb.","ε = ΔΦ/Δt = 7.54 × 10⁻⁷/0.1 = 7.54 × 10⁻⁶ V."],"a":"7.54 × 10⁻⁶ V"},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",21]],"s":["ε = Blv, where l is the side that is leaving the field (perpendicular to v).","(a) v normal to the longer side: l = 0.08 m. ε = 0.3 × 0.08 × 0.01 = 2.4 × 10⁻⁴ V; the loop must move 2 cm to leave, so t = 0.02/0.01 = 2 s.","(b) v normal to the shorter side: l = 0.02 m. ε = 0.3 × 0.02 × 0.01 = 0.6 × 10⁻⁴ V; it must move 8 cm, so t = 8 s."],"a":"(a) 2.4 × 10⁻⁴ V for 2 s (b) 0.6 × 10⁻⁴ V for 8 s"},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",22]],"s":["ε = BLv = 0.080 × 0.090 × 35 = 0.252 V.","I = ε/R = 0.252/(5.00 × 10⁻³) = 50.4 A.","Direction: v up, B to the right; q v × B for a positive charge points into the page, so the induced current flows into the page (perpendicular to both)."],"a":"50.4 A, directed into the page"},{"q":[["phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",23]],"s":["Change of flux linkage in the second coil: Δ(NΦ) = M ΔI.","Δ(NΦ) = 1.5 × (20 − 0)","Δ(NΦ) = 30 Wb (weber-turns)"],"a":"30 Wb"}]}},"15":{"items":{"mcqs":[{"q":[["phys15-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-8-196-197",14]],"s":["I₀ = √2 × I_rms = 1.414 × 100","I₀ = 141.4 A"],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys15-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-8-196-197",15]],"s":["ω = 2πf = 2π × 50","ω = 100π rad s⁻¹"],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys15-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-8-196-197",16]],"s":["In a purely resistive circuit current and voltage are in phase, φ = 0.","Power factor cos φ = cos 0 = 1 (unity)."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys15-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-8-196-197",17]],"s":["X_C = 1/(2πfC); for direct current f = 0, so X_C is infinite.","Once charged, a capacitor blocks d.c. completely but passes a.c."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys15-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-8-196-197",18]],"s":["I_rms = I₀/√2 = 5√2/√2 = 5 A.","The mean square value is the mean of I², i.e. I_rms² = 5² = 25 A². So the answer is D (5²)."],"n":"The r.m.s. value is 5 A (option A); the question asks for the mean square value, which is 5² = 25 A².","inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys15-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-8-196-197",19]],"s":["A choke controls a.c. with very little power loss, so it must have a large inductive reactance and a very small resistance.","X_L ≫ R."],"n":"The book prints the label (D) twice; the third choice (X_L ≫ R) is taken as option C.","inline":1,"r":[["(D)","(C)"]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys15-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-8-196-197",20]],"s":["At resonance X_L = X_C, so the impedance is purely resistive (Z = R).","Current and voltage are in phase: phase difference 0."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys15-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-8-196-197",21]],"s":["A sine wave is the natural output of a rotating-coil generator and is the only waveform whose shape is unchanged by inductors, capacitors and transformers.","It produces the least disturbance (distortion, harmonics, interference) in electrical circuits."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",0]],"s":["V = V₀ sin ωt with ω = 157 rad s⁻¹.","f = ω/2π = 157/6.28 = 25 Hz."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",1]],"s":["X_L = 2πfL, so inductive reactance increases in proportion to frequency (R does not depend on frequency)."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",2]],"s":["An alternating quantity v = V₀ sin(ωt + φ) is completely known when its maximum value V₀, its frequency and its phase φ are known."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",3]],"s":["Maxwell added the displacement current ε₀ dΦ_E/dt to Ampere's law: ∮B·dl = μ₀(I + ε₀ dΦ_E/dt).","This generalised Ampere's law led to the prediction of electromagnetic waves."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",4]],"s":["When an electromagnetic wave passes from air into glass its speed and wavelength change, but its frequency does not change.","Of the choices, radio waves, X-rays and ultraviolet are all electromagnetic waves; ultrasound is a mechanical (sound) wave, not electromagnetic."],"n":"The printed choices do not match the stem (they list radiations, not quantities). The quantity that does not change is the frequency; read as 'which is not an electromagnetic wave', the answer is D (ultrasound).","inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",5]],"s":["Current and voltage in phase means φ = 0.","Power factor cos 0 = 1."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",6]],"s":["Z = √(R² + (X_L − X_C)²) with X_L = 2πfL rising and X_C = 1/(2πfC) falling as f increases.","Below the resonant frequency Z decreases as f rises; at resonance Z = R (minimum); above resonance Z increases.","So the impedance neither always increases nor always decreases; X_C decreases while X_L increases."],"n":"No single printed choice is correct for every frequency (the label (D) is also printed twice). Above resonance the impedance increases (B); below resonance it decreases (C).","inline":1,"r":[["(D)","(C)"]]}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",8]],"s":["ε = −L ΔI/Δt, so a graph of induced e.m.f. against rate of change of current is a straight line through the origin (with negative slope, since the e.m.f. opposes the change).","The magnitude of the gradient equals the self-inductance L of the coil."]},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",9]],"s":["For a resistor the current and voltage are in phase: they change sign together.","Instantaneous power p = vi = V₀I₀ sin²ωt is the product of two quantities of the same sign, so it is always positive (or zero).","Energy always flows from the generator into the resistor; average power = V_rmsI_rms."]},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",10]],"s":["X_L = 2πfL, so a graph of X_L against f is a straight line through the origin.","Its gradient is 2πL, determined by the inductance L of the coil."]},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",11]],"s":["(a) X_L = 2πfL ∝ f: doubling the frequency doubles the inductive reactance.","(b) X_C = 1/(2πfC) ∝ 1/f: doubling the frequency halves the capacitive reactance."]},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",12]],"s":["V_rms = V₀/√2 = 1000/1.414","V_rms = 707 V"]},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",13]],"s":["Inductor: X_L = ωL. Unit: s⁻¹ × H = s⁻¹ × V s A⁻¹ = V A⁻¹ = Ω.","Capacitor: X_C = 1/(ωC). Unit: 1/(s⁻¹ × F) = 1/(s⁻¹ × C V⁻¹) = V s C⁻¹ = V A⁻¹ = Ω.","So both reactances are measured in ohms."]},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",14]],"s":["The heart muscle produces small electrical potentials as it contracts and relaxes (depolarisation and repolarisation of its cells).","These potential differences (about 1 mV) spread through the body and are picked up by electrodes placed on the skin.","An electrocardiograph amplifies and records them against time; the trace (P wave, QRS complex, T wave) shows the heart's rhythm and helps diagnose heart disease."]}],"longQuestions":[{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",16]],"s":["For i = I₀ sin ωt: the peak value I₀ is the maximum value in a cycle; the mean value over a full cycle is zero, and over a half cycle I_mean = 2I₀/π = 0.637 I₀.","The r.m.s. (effective) value is the steady d.c. current that would produce the same heating in a resistor: I_rms = √(mean of i²).","i² = I₀² sin²ωt = I₀²(1 − cos 2ωt)/2. Over a cycle the mean of cos 2ωt is zero, so mean of i² = I₀²/2.","I_rms = I₀/√2 = 0.707 I₀. Similarly V_rms = V₀/√2 and V_mean (half cycle) = 2V₀/π."]},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",17]],"s":["Instantaneous voltage: v = V₀ sin ωt.","By Ohm's law the instantaneous current is i = v/R = (V₀/R) sin ωt = I₀ sin ωt; current is in phase with voltage (φ = 0).","Instantaneous power p = vi = V₀I₀ sin²ωt, always positive.","Average power per cycle: P = V₀I₀ × mean(sin²ωt) = V₀I₀/2 = V_rmsI_rms = I_rms²R. The phasors V and I point in the same direction."]},{"q":[["phys15-exercise-mcqs-9-15-conceptual-and-comprehensive-198-199",18]],"s":["Instantaneous voltage: v = V₀ sin ωt; charge on the capacitor q = Cv = CV₀ sin ωt.","Current i = dq/dt = ωCV₀ cos ωt = I₀ sin(ωt + π/2), with I₀ = V₀/X_C and X_C = 1/(ωC). The current leads the voltage by 90°.","Instantaneous power p = vi = V₀I₀ sin ωt cos ωt = ½V₀I₀ sin 2ωt, which is positive for one quarter cycle (capacitor charging) and negative for the next (returning energy).","Average power per cycle = 0: a pure capacitor dissipates no energy."]},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",0]],"s":["Impedance Z is the total opposition of an a.c. circuit to current, combining resistance and reactance: Z = V_rms/I_rms, measured in ohms.","In a series RLC circuit the same current I flows through all; V_R = IR is in phase with I, V_L = IX_L leads I by 90°, V_C = IX_C lags I by 90°.","From the phasor diagram: V = √(V_R² + (V_L − V_C)²) = I√(R² + (X_L − X_C)²).","Z = √(R² + (X_L − X_C)²), with phase angle tan φ = (X_L − X_C)/R.","At resonance X_L = X_C, Z = R (minimum) and f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC))."]},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",1]],"s":["A source of e.m.f. ε with internal resistance r supplies a load R: I = ε/(R + r), and the power in the load is P = I²R = ε²R/(R + r)².","For maximum P, dP/dR = 0: ε²[(R + r)² − 2R(R + r)]/(R + r)⁴ = 0, which gives R = r.","So maximum power is delivered when the load resistance equals the internal resistance; then P_max = ε²/(4r) (efficiency 50%).","For a.c. circuits the same result holds with impedances: maximum power is transferred when the load impedance matches the source impedance (conjugate match, e.g. a transformer used to match a loudspeaker to an amplifier)."]},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",2]],"s":["In a series RL circuit the current lags the applied voltage.","Take the current I as reference phasor. V_R = IR is in phase with I; V_L = IX_L leads I by 90°.","The applied voltage V is the phasor sum: V = √(V_R² + V_L²) = I√(R² + X_L²), so Z = √(R² + X_L²).","V is ahead of I by angle φ with tan φ = X_L/R = ωL/R. Thus the current lags the voltage by φ (0 < φ < 90°)."]},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",3]],"s":["In a series RC circuit the current leads the applied voltage.","Take the current I as reference. V_R = IR is in phase with I; V_C = IX_C lags I by 90°.","Phasor sum: V = √(V_R² + V_C²) = I√(R² + X_C²), so Z = √(R² + X_C²).","V is behind I by angle φ with tan φ = X_C/R = 1/(ωCR). So the current leads the voltage by φ (0 < φ < 90°)."]},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",4]],"s":["Maxwell's equations: (1) Gauss's law for electricity: ∮E·dA = Q/ε₀. (2) Gauss's law for magnetism: ∮B·dA = 0 (no isolated poles). (3) Faraday's law: ∮E·dl = −dΦ_B/dt. (4) Ampere–Maxwell law: ∮B·dl = μ₀(I + ε₀ dΦ_E/dt).","Induced electric field: a changing magnetic flux through a circular path of radius r induces an e.m.f. ε = ΔΦ/Δt round it. Since ε = ∮E·dl = E(2πr), E = (1/2πr)(ΔΦ/Δt). With Φ = AB, E = (A/2πr)(ΔB/Δt).","Displacement current: between capacitor plates no charge flows, but the electric flux Φ_E = EA = q/ε₀ changes. Then Δq/Δt = ε₀ ΔΦ_E/Δt, so the displacement current is I_d = ε₀ ΔΦ_E/Δt, which completes the circuit for Ampere's law.","Together these show that changing E and B fields generate each other, giving electromagnetic waves moving at c = 1/√(μ₀ε₀)."]},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",5]],"s":["A metal detector uses electromagnetic induction. A transmitter coil carries an alternating (or pulsed) current and produces a changing magnetic field.","When a metal object comes into this field, eddy currents are induced in it; these produce their own magnetic field.","This secondary field induces a small voltage in a receiver coil (or changes the inductance/frequency of the search coil).","The electronic circuit detects the change and gives a sound or signal. Used at airports, for security checks, and to locate buried pipes, mines and coins."]}],"numericals":[{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",7]],"s":["V_rms = V₀/√2 = 300/1.414","V_rms = 212.1 V"],"a":"212.1 V"},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",8]],"s":["I₀ = √2 × I_rms = 1.414 × 10","I₀ = 14.1 A"],"a":"14.1 A"},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",9]],"s":["ω = 2 × 10³ rad s⁻¹, C = 0.5 × 10⁻⁶ F.","(a) X_C = 1/(ωC) = 1/(2 × 10³ × 0.5 × 10⁻⁶) = 1000 Ω.","(b) I₀ = V₀/X_C = 16/1000 = 0.016 A = 16 mA."],"a":"(a) 1000 Ω (b) 16 mA"},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",10]],"s":["X_C = 1/(ωC) = 1/(1.25 × 10⁴ × 0.01 × 10⁻⁶) = 8000 Ω.","I₀ = V₀/X_C = 240/8000 = 0.03 A.","In a capacitor the current leads the voltage by 90°: −30° + 90° = 60°.","i = 0.03 sin(1.25 × 10⁴t + 60°) A"],"a":"i = 0.03 sin(1.25 × 10⁴t + 60°) A"},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",11]],"s":["X_L = V/I = 6.3/0.010 = 630 Ω.","X_L = 2πfL, so f = X_L/(2πL) = 630/(2π × 100 × 10⁻⁶)","f = 1.0 × 10⁶ Hz"],"a":"≈ 10⁶ Hz (1 MHz)"},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",12]],"s":["(a) X_L = 2πfL. At 60 Hz: 2π × 60 × 3.00 × 10⁻³ = 1.13 Ω. At 10.0 kHz: 2π × 10⁴ × 3.00 × 10⁻³ = 188 Ω.","(b) I_rms = V_rms/X_L: at 60 Hz 120/1.13 = 106 A; at 10 kHz 120/188 = 0.637 A."],"a":"(a) 1.13 Ω, 188 Ω (b) 106 A, 0.637 A"},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",13]],"s":["(a) f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC)) = 1/(2π√(3.00 × 10⁻³ × 5.00 × 10⁻⁶)) = 1/(2π × 1.225 × 10⁻⁴)","f₀ = 1.30 × 10³ Hz = 1.30 kHz.","(b) At resonance Z = R: I_rms = 120/40.0 = 3.00 A."],"a":"(a) 1.30 kHz (b) 3.00 A"},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",14]],"s":["X_C = 1/(2πfC) = 1/(2π × 50 × 79.5 × 10⁻⁶) = 40 Ω.","(a) Z = √(R² + X_C²) = √(30² + 40²) = 50 Ω.","(b) I_rms = 100/50 = 2 A.","(c) tan φ = X_C/R = 40/30, φ = 53.1°, current leading.","(d) I₀ = 2√2 = 2.828 A, ω = 314 rad s⁻¹: i = 2.828 sin(314t + 53°) A."],"a":"Z = 50 Ω, I = 2 A, φ = 53° lead, i = 2.828 sin(314t + 53°) A"},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",15]],"s":["X_L = 2πfL = 2π × 50 × 31.8 × 10⁻³ = 10.0 Ω.","Z = √(7² + 10²) = 12.2 Ω.","(a) I = 230/12.2 = 18.85 A.","(b) tan φ = 10/7, φ = 55° (current lagging).","(c) Power factor = cos φ = R/Z = 7/12.2 = 0.573 (lagging).","(d) P = I²R = 18.85² × 7 = 2.48 × 10³ W (≈ 2484 W)."],"a":"18.85 A, 55° lag, 0.573 lag, ≈ 2484 W","n":"The printed power factor '.573⁰' carries a stray degree sign; a power factor has no unit."},{"q":[["phys15-comprehensive-4-9-and-numericals-1-10-200-201",16]],"s":["X_C = 1/(ωC) = 1/(314 × 100 × 10⁻⁶) = 31.85 Ω.","Z = √(R² + X_C²) = √(10² + 31.85²) = 33.4 Ω.","I₀ = V₀/Z = 100/33.4 = 3.0 A."],"a":"X_C = 31.85 Ω, Z = 33.4 Ω, I₀ ≈ 3 A"}]}},"16":{"items":{"mcqs":[{"q":[["phys16-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-9-226-227",13]],"s":["Shear modulus G = shear stress/shear strain.","Shear stress = F/A and shear strain = angle θ, so G = F/(Aθ)."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys16-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-9-226-227",14]],"s":["Below a certain temperature the resistance of a superconductor drops suddenly to zero.","This temperature is called the critical (transition) temperature T_c."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys16-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-9-226-227",15]],"s":["Iron, nickel and cobalt are ferromagnetic.","Barium is not ferromagnetic."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys16-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-9-226-227",16]],"s":["By Hooke's law, within the elastic limit stress ∝ strain.","Their ratio is a constant — the modulus of elasticity of the material."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys16-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-9-226-227",17]],"s":["Strain = ΔL/L. Doubling the length gives ΔL = L.","Strain = L/L = 1."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys16-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-9-226-227",18]],"s":["Compressing a rod along its length changes its length; the modulus involved is the ratio of longitudinal stress to longitudinal strain.","That is Young's modulus (the same as for stretching)."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys16-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-9-226-227",19]],"s":["Strain = ΔL/L = L/L = 1 when the wire is stretched to double its length."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys16-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-9-226-227",20]],"s":["The modulus of elasticity is a property of the material only, not of the dimensions of the rod.","Doubling length and radius changes the extension for a given load, but the modulus remains the same."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys16-key-points-and-exercise-mcqs-1-9-226-227",21]],"s":["Above the Curie temperature the thermal agitation destroys the alignment of the domains.","A ferromagnetic material then becomes paramagnetic."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",0]],"s":["The cable breaks at a definite breaking stress, i.e. at a definite strain ΔL/L.","Each half has length L/2, so the maximum safe extension is half: 2 mm/2 = 1 mm."],"inline":1,"c":1}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",3]],"s":["Coercive force is the reverse magnetising field needed to reduce the magnetisation of a material to zero.","Steel is a hard magnetic material: carbon atoms and lattice defects pin the domain walls, so the domains are difficult to rotate back. It has a wide hysteresis loop and a large coercive force.","Soft iron is pure and its domains turn easily, so its coercive force is small. This is why steel is used for permanent magnets and soft iron for electromagnets."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",4]],"s":["Strength is the maximum stress a material can bear before breaking (ultimate tensile strength); stiffness is its resistance to deformation, measured by Young's modulus.","In design both are needed: the structure must not break under the largest expected load (strength, with a safety factor), and it must not bend, sag or vibrate excessively under working loads (stiffness).","For example, bridge girders and building beams use steel, which is both strong and stiff; a material that is strong but not stiff (rubber) or stiff but brittle (glass) is unsuitable for such members."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",5]],"s":["Superconductivity is the complete loss of electrical resistance of a material below its critical temperature T_c.","Graph of resistance against temperature: as the temperature falls, R decreases gradually, then at T_c it drops suddenly to zero and stays zero.","Example: mercury becomes superconducting at 4.2 K; some ceramic (copper-oxide) materials at about 125 K. A current set up in a superconducting ring flows indefinitely without loss."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",6]],"s":["Crystalline solids: atoms or ions are arranged in a regular, repeating three-dimensional pattern (long-range order); they have sharp melting points and definite shapes, e.g. NaCl, metals, quartz.","Amorphous solids: no long-range order (only short-range order), like a frozen liquid; they soften over a range of temperature, e.g. glass, pitch.","Polymeric solids: made of very long chain molecules formed by repeated monomer units, partly crystalline and partly amorphous; light and flexible, e.g. polythene, nylon, rubber."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",7]],"s":["Elastic deformation: the solid regains its original shape and size when the deforming force is removed; it occurs below the elastic limit and obeys Hooke's law.","Plastic deformation: beyond the elastic limit (yield point) the solid is permanently deformed and does not return to its original shape when the force is removed."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",8]],"s":["Paramagnetic: atoms have permanent magnetic moments that are randomly oriented; weakly attracted by a magnet and slightly magnetised along the field; e.g. aluminium, platinum, oxygen.","Diamagnetic: atoms have no net permanent moment; an external field induces a weak opposite moment, so they are weakly repelled; e.g. copper, bismuth, water.","Ferromagnetic: permanent moments align strongly in domains; strongly attracted, can be magnetised strongly and may retain magnetism; e.g. iron, nickel, cobalt."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",9]],"s":["Soft magnetic substances (e.g. soft iron) have a narrow, tall hysteresis loop: low coercivity and low retentivity, so they magnetise and demagnetise easily with little energy loss — used for transformer cores and electromagnets.","Hard magnetic substances (e.g. steel, alnico) have a wide hysteresis loop: high coercivity and retentivity, so they keep their magnetism — used for permanent magnets.","(Curves: B against H; the soft-iron loop encloses a small area, the steel loop a large area.)"]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",10]],"s":["Brittle materials break suddenly just after the elastic limit, with little or no plastic deformation, e.g. glass, cast iron, ceramics.","Ductile materials undergo large plastic deformation before breaking and can be drawn into wires, e.g. copper, gold, aluminium."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",11]],"s":["By doping: adding a small amount of a pentavalent impurity (P, As) gives extra free electrons (n-type) and a trivalent impurity (B, Ga) gives holes (p-type).","By raising the temperature or shining light on it, which breaks more covalent bonds and creates more electron–hole pairs."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",12]],"s":["Yes. Standing vertically, each part of the girder supports the weight of the part above it, so it is compressed by its own weight and becomes slightly shorter.","Lying horizontally on supports there is no such lengthwise compression.","The difference is very small: ΔL = ρgL²/(2Y) ≈ (7800 × 9.8 × 20²)/(2 × 2 × 10¹¹) ≈ 8 × 10⁻⁵ m."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",13]],"s":["An MRI machine needs a very strong, steady and uniform magnetic field (about 1.5–3 T), which requires very large currents in its coils.","Superconducting coils have zero resistance, so these large currents flow with no I²R heating and no power loss; once started, the current persists and the field stays very stable.","Ordinary copper coils would need enormous power and cooling to produce such fields."]}],"longQuestions":[{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",15]],"s":["A tensile force stretches a body (pulls its ends apart), producing tensile stress and an increase in length; a compressive force squeezes it, producing compressive stress and a decrease in length.","In a structure (bridge, truss, building) members carry either tension (ties, cables) or compression (columns, struts); in a loaded beam the top is in compression and the bottom in tension.","For equilibrium the resultant force and resultant torque on every member and joint must be zero: the internal tensile and compressive forces balance the external loads.","Materials are chosen accordingly: steel is strong in tension, concrete is strong in compression but weak in tension (hence reinforced concrete). If a member's stress exceeds its strength it fails and equilibrium is lost."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",16]],"s":["The stress–strain graph of a ductile metal (e.g. copper) is drawn with strain on the x-axis and stress on the y-axis.","OA: straight line — stress ∝ strain (Hooke's law), A is the proportional limit. Up to B (elastic limit) the wire returns to its original length: this is the elastic region.","Beyond B the material yields: at the yield point (yield stress) the strain increases with little increase in stress — plastic flow region; the deformation is permanent.","Stress then rises to a maximum at C: the ultimate tensile stress (strength). After C the wire narrows (necks) and breaks at the breaking point D.","Young's modulus is the gradient of the linear part OA."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",17]],"s":["In a solid, the energy levels of the many atoms merge into energy bands.","Valence band: the band occupied by the valence electrons; at 0 K it is completely filled.","Conduction band: the next higher band; electrons in it are free to move and conduct electricity.","Forbidden energy gap E_g: the gap between the top of the valence band and the bottom of the conduction band, in which no electron states exist.","Conductors: the bands overlap (E_g = 0). Insulators: large gap (> 3 eV, e.g. diamond ≈ 6 eV). Semiconductors: small gap (Si ≈ 1.1 eV, Ge ≈ 0.7 eV), so some electrons cross it at room temperature, leaving holes in the valence band."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",18]],"s":["Superconductivity is the state in which a material has zero electrical resistance below a critical temperature T_c (discovered by Kamerlingh Onnes in mercury, T_c = 4.2 K).","Curve: plotting resistance against temperature, R falls steadily on cooling and then drops abruptly to zero at T_c.","A superconductor also expels magnetic field from its interior (Meissner effect) and loses superconductivity in a strong enough magnetic field.","High-temperature superconductors (copper-oxide ceramics) have T_c above 77 K, so they can be cooled with liquid nitrogen.","Uses: MRI magnets, maglev trains, loss-free power cables, particle-accelerator magnets, SQUIDs."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",19]],"s":["Mechanical properties describe how a solid responds to forces.","Elasticity: the ability to regain shape after deformation. Plasticity: permanent deformation beyond the elastic limit.","Stress = F/A, strain = ΔL/L; their ratio within the elastic limit is the modulus of elasticity: Young's modulus Y (length), bulk modulus K (volume), shear modulus G (shape).","Strength (ultimate tensile stress), stiffness (Young's modulus), ductility (drawn into wires), brittleness (breaks without plastic deformation), hardness and toughness.","These properties are found from the stress–strain graph and decide the use of a material in engineering."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",20]],"s":["When a ferromagnetic material is taken through a cycle of magnetisation, B is plotted against the magnetising field H (or current).","Starting from zero, B rises along a curve to saturation. When H is reduced to zero, B does not return to zero: the remaining B is the remanence (retentivity).","A reverse field H_c (coercive force) is needed to bring B to zero; further increase gives saturation in the opposite direction, and the cycle completes a closed loop.","This lag of B behind H is hysteresis. The area of the loop equals the energy lost as heat per unit volume per cycle.","For soft iron the loop is narrow (small remanence and coercivity, small loss) — ideal for transformer cores; steel gives a wide loop."]},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",21]],"s":["When a wire is stretched by a force, work is done on it and stored as elastic potential (strain) energy.","Within the elastic limit the force increases uniformly from 0 to F as the extension goes from 0 to ΔL; the force–extension graph is a straight line.","Work done = area under the graph = ½ F ΔL. So strain energy U = ½ F ΔL.","Dividing by volume AL: energy per unit volume = ½ (F/A)(ΔL/L) = ½ × stress × strain = ½ Y (strain)².","If the wire is stretched beyond the elastic limit, part of the work is lost as heat and the area under the loading curve is not all recovered."]}],"numericals":[{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",23]],"s":["Take Y(steel) = 2.0 × 10¹¹ N m⁻², g = 9.8 m s⁻².","A = πr² = π(0.125 × 10⁻² m)² = 4.91 × 10⁻⁶ m²; F = mg = 98 N.","With L = 1.50 m: ΔL = FL/(AY) = (98 × 1.50)/(4.91 × 10⁻⁶ × 2.0 × 10¹¹) = 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ m."],"a":"≈ 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ m (for a 1.50 m wire)","n":"The book prints the length as 1.50 cm, but its answer 1.5 × 10⁻⁴ m corresponds to a 1.50 m wire; a 1.50 cm wire would stretch only 1.5 × 10⁻⁶ m."},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",24]],"s":["A = π(0.01)² = 3.14 × 10⁻⁴ m².","(a) Stress = F/A = 1 × 10⁴/3.14 × 10⁻⁴ = 3.18 × 10⁷ N m⁻² ≈ 0.32 × 10⁸ N m⁻².","(b) Strain = stress/Y = 3.18 × 10⁷/2.0 × 10¹¹ = 1.6 × 10⁻⁴.","(c) ΔL = strain × L = 1.6 × 10⁻⁴ × 0.50 = 0.8 × 10⁻⁴ m."],"a":"(a) 3.2 × 10⁷ N m⁻² (b) 1.6 × 10⁻⁴ (c) 0.8 × 10⁻⁴ m","n":"Y for steel taken as 2.0 × 10¹¹ N m⁻²."},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",25]],"s":["Strain = ΔL/L = 1.2 × 10⁻⁴/12 = 1.0 × 10⁻⁵.","Energy per unit volume = ½ × stress × strain = ½ × 6.4 × 10⁸ × 1.0 × 10⁻⁵","= 3.2 × 10³ J m⁻³"],"a":"3.2 × 10³ J m⁻³"},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",26]],"s":["Weight W = mg = 100 × 9.8 = 980 N, shared equally by four legs: F = 245 N per leg (area 2 × 10⁻³ m²).","(a) Stress per leg = 245/(2 × 10⁻³) = 1.2 × 10⁵ N m⁻².","(b) Strain = stress/Y = 1.2 × 10⁵/1 × 10¹⁰ = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵; shrinkage ΔL = 1.2 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.42 = 5.1 × 10⁻⁶ m."],"a":"(a) ≈ 1.2 × 10⁵ N m⁻² (b) ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁶ m","n":"The printed answers (5 × 10⁵ N m⁻², 2.1 × 10⁻⁵ m) put Hamza's whole weight (≈1000 N) on one leg. With the weight shared by four legs, each value is one quarter. The area unit is printed as m instead of m²."},{"q":[["phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",27]],"s":["(a) Stress = F/A = 1.5 × 10⁴/4.8 × 10⁻⁴ = 3.13 × 10⁷ N m⁻². Y = stress/strain = 3.13 × 10⁷/1.4 × 10⁻⁴ = 2.2 × 10¹¹ N m⁻².","(b) Linear graph: energy per unit volume = ½ Y (strain)² = ½ × 2.23 × 10¹¹ × (1 × 10⁻⁴)²","= 1.1 × 10³ J m⁻³"],"a":"(a) 2.2 × 10¹¹ N m⁻² (b) ≈ 1.1 × 10³ J m⁻³","n":"The book prints 1.5 × 10³ J m⁻³ for (b); with Y = 2.2 × 10¹¹ N m⁻² and strain 1 × 10⁻⁴ the energy density is 1.1 × 10³ J m⁻³."}]}},"17":{"items":{"mcqs":[{"q":[["phys17-keypoints-mcq1-8-conceptual1-9-comprehensive1-250-251",11]],"s":["N-type silicon is made by adding pentavalent (donor) atoms such as P or As, which supply extra free electrons.","So electrons are majority carriers and holes are minority carriers, with pentavalent dopants — option C."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys17-keypoints-mcq1-8-conceptual1-9-comprehensive1-250-251",12]],"s":["The barrier potential of a silicon junction at room temperature is about 0.7 V (about 0.3 V for germanium)."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys17-keypoints-mcq1-8-conceptual1-9-comprehensive1-250-251",13]],"s":["Reverse bias pulls majority carriers away from the junction, uncovering more ions.","So the depletion region widens under reverse bias (and narrows under forward bias)."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys17-keypoints-mcq1-8-conceptual1-9-comprehensive1-250-251",14]],"s":["In the common-emitter configuration the input current is I_B and the output current is I_C.","Current gain β = I_C/I_B."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys17-keypoints-mcq1-8-conceptual1-9-comprehensive1-250-251",15]],"s":["Under reverse bias the majority carriers cannot cross the barrier; the small current is carried by thermally generated minority carriers.","So the reverse saturation current is due to minority carriers."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys17-keypoints-mcq1-8-conceptual1-9-comprehensive1-250-251",16]],"s":["Under reverse bias practically no current flows (only a tiny leakage current), so the junction behaves like an OFF (open) switch.","The depletion layer between two conducting regions also gives it a junction capacitance (used in varactor diodes)."],"n":"Some keys give A (capacitor) because a reverse-biased junction has capacitance; as a circuit element it acts as an OFF switch.","inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys17-keypoints-mcq1-8-conceptual1-9-comprehensive1-250-251",17]],"s":["Wrong bias shifts the operating (Q) point towards saturation or cut-off.","Part of the signal is then clipped, producing distortion in the output signal."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys17-keypoints-mcq1-8-conceptual1-9-comprehensive1-250-251",18]],"s":["In digital circuits the transistor works as a switch: fully ON (saturation) or fully OFF (cut-off).","So it operates in the saturation and cut-off regions."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",0]],"s":["The base is thin and lightly doped, so only a few electrons recombine there (forming I_B).","Most electrons from the emitter cross the base into the collector."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",1]],"s":["In the active region I_C = βI_B.","The collector current is controlled by the base current (practically independent of collector voltage)."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",2]],"s":["A centre-tapped full-wave rectifier uses 2 diodes (a bridge rectifier uses 4).","The minimum number is 2."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",3]],"s":["With both junctions reverse biased no carriers are injected from the emitter.","I_C ≈ 0: the transistor is in the cut-off region."],"inline":1,"c":1}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",5]],"s":["(a) The base is made thin and lightly doped so that very few carriers coming from the emitter recombine in it. Almost all of them reach the collector, giving a small base current and a large current gain.","(b) The collector is made large because the collector–base junction is reverse biased at a higher voltage and most of the power (≈ I_CV_CE) is dissipated there; a large collector dissipates heat and collects the carriers efficiently."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",6]],"s":["The base is very thin and lightly doped, so only about 1–5% of the carriers injected from the emitter recombine with the few carriers in the base.","These recombinations form the small base current I_B; the remaining 95–99% are swept into the collector as I_C. So I_B ≪ I_C (I_C = βI_B)."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",7]],"s":["Forward biasing the emitter–base junction lowers its barrier, so the emitter injects a large number of majority carriers into the base.","Reverse biasing the collector–base junction creates a strong field that sweeps the carriers reaching the base into the collector.","This bias (active region) is needed for normal transistor action and amplification."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",8]],"s":["NPN: the forward-biased emitter–base junction injects electrons from the N-type emitter into the thin P-type base. A few recombine with holes (base current); most diffuse across and are swept by the reverse-biased collector junction into the collector. I_E = I_B + I_C.","PNP: the same action occurs with holes as the majority carriers injected from the P-type emitter, and all bias polarities and current directions are reversed.","In both, a small base current controls a large collector current."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",9]],"s":["N-type: intrinsic Si/Ge doped with a pentavalent (donor) impurity such as P, As, Sb; electrons are majority carriers, holes minority; donor ions are positive.","P-type: doped with a trivalent (acceptor) impurity such as B, Al, Ga, In; holes are majority carriers, electrons minority; acceptor ions are negative."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",10]],"s":["The P-type material is made of neutral silicon atoms and neutral trivalent impurity atoms; doping adds no net charge.","Each hole created by an acceptor atom is balanced by the negative charge of that ionised acceptor. Total positive charge (holes) equals total negative charge (acceptor ions + electrons), so the crystal is electrically neutral."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",11]],"s":["The common-emitter amplifier gives a high current gain (β ≈ 50–300), a high voltage gain and the highest power gain of the three configurations.","Its input resistance is moderate and its output resistance moderately high, so stages can be cascaded easily. Hence CE is the most widely used amplifier configuration."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",12]],"s":["A small change in the input (base) current produces a large change in the output (collector) current: ΔI_C = β ΔI_B, with β typically 100 or more.","Thus the transistor amplifies current, and with a load resistor this also gives voltage and power amplification."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",13]],"s":["Each trivalent atom accepts an electron from a neighbouring Si bond and creates one hole: 10 trivalent atoms give 10 holes.","Thermal generation at 25 °C is negligible for so few atoms (intrinsic carriers ≈ 1.5 × 10¹⁰ per 5 × 10²² atoms, i.e. about 0.003 pairs for 10¹⁰ atoms).","So there are about 10 holes and practically no free electrons."]}],"longQuestions":[{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",15]],"s":["In a solid the energy levels of the atoms broaden into bands; the important ones are the valence band and the conduction band, separated by the forbidden energy gap E_g.","Insulator: the valence band is full, the conduction band empty, and E_g is large (> 3 eV, e.g. diamond ≈ 6 eV); electrons cannot be excited across it, so there is no conduction.","Semiconductor: E_g is small (Si 1.1 eV, Ge 0.7 eV). At 0 K it is an insulator; at room temperature some electrons jump into the conduction band, leaving holes, so it conducts moderately; conductivity rises with temperature.","Conductor: the valence and conduction bands overlap (no gap) or the band is partly filled, so plenty of free electrons are available and conductivity is high."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",16]],"s":["When P and N regions are joined, electrons diffuse from N to P and holes from P to N, where they recombine.","This leaves a thin region near the junction with no free carriers but with uncovered ions: positive donor ions on the N side and negative acceptor ions on the P side — the depletion layer.","These ions set up an electric field and a potential barrier (≈ 0.7 V for Si, 0.3 V for Ge) that stops further diffusion; equilibrium is reached when diffusion and drift balance.","Energy band diagram: the bands on the P side are raised relative to the N side by eV₀; the Fermi level is the same throughout, and electrons need energy eV₀ to cross.","Significance: the depletion layer makes the junction conduct in one direction only — forward bias narrows it (conduction), reverse bias widens it (blocking). It also gives the junction its capacitance."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",17]],"s":["A half-wave rectifier converts a.c. into d.c. using one diode in series with a load resistor R_L across the secondary of a transformer.","During the positive half cycle the diode is forward biased; it conducts and current flows through R_L, giving an output voltage.","During the negative half cycle the diode is reverse biased; it does not conduct and the output is zero.","The output is a series of positive half-cycle pulses (pulsating d.c.) in one direction; a capacitor filter can smooth it."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",18]],"s":["An NPN transistor in common-emitter mode: the emitter–base junction is forward biased (V_BB) and the collector–base junction reverse biased (V_CC) through a load resistor R_C. The input signal is applied between base and emitter.","The signal changes the base current slightly by ΔI_B; this produces a large change in collector current ΔI_C = βΔI_B.","The output voltage V_CE = V_CC − I_CR_C therefore changes by ΔV_out = −βΔI_B R_C, much larger than the input change.","Voltage gain A_v = −β R_C/r_i; the negative sign means the output is 180° out of phase with the input. In this way a weak signal is amplified."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",19]],"s":["Half-wave rectifier: a.c. source (transformer secondary) → one diode in series → load resistor R_L. Output: positive half cycles only.","Full-wave (centre-tap) rectifier: transformer with centre-tapped secondary; two diodes D₁ and D₂ connected to the two ends, their outputs joined at one end of R_L, the other end of R_L to the centre tap. Each diode conducts on alternate half cycles, so current flows through R_L in the same direction during both halves.","Full-wave bridge rectifier: four diodes in a bridge; two conduct in each half cycle. (Circuit diagrams as in the textbook figures.)"]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",20]],"s":["Half-wave rectifier — advantages: simple, cheap, only one diode, no centre-tapped transformer. Disadvantages: uses only half of each cycle, low efficiency (≈ 40.6%), large ripple (ripple frequency = f), output more difficult to smooth.","Full-wave rectifier — advantages: uses both halves of the cycle, higher average output (2V₀/π) and efficiency (≈ 81.2%), smaller ripple at twice the supply frequency, easier to filter.","Disadvantages of full-wave: needs two diodes and a centre-tapped transformer (or four diodes in a bridge), so it is more costly; each diode must withstand a larger peak inverse voltage in the centre-tap type."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",21]],"s":["Definitions: α = I_C/I_E (common-base current gain) and β = I_C/I_B (common-emitter current gain).","Currents: I_E = I_B + I_C.","Divide by I_C: I_E/I_C = I_B/I_C + 1, i.e. 1/α = 1/β + 1.","Hence β = α/(1 − α) and α = β/(1 + β). Since α is slightly less than 1 (e.g. 0.98), β is large (e.g. 49)."]},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",22]],"s":["An extrinsic semiconductor is a pure semiconductor doped with a small amount of impurity, which greatly increases its conductivity.","N-type: a pentavalent donor introduces an energy level just below the conduction band (≈ 0.01–0.05 eV). At room temperature nearly all donor electrons are excited into the conduction band, so electrons are majority carriers.","P-type: a trivalent acceptor introduces an energy level just above the valence band; valence electrons easily move into it, leaving holes — the majority carriers.","The band gap of the host (Si 1.1 eV) is unchanged, but the small donor/acceptor ionisation energy means many carriers are available, so conductivity σ = e(nμ_e + pμ_h) is much higher than in the intrinsic material and is controlled by the doping level."]}],"numericals":[{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",24]],"s":["β = I_C/I_B = 10 × 10⁻³/40 × 10⁻⁶","β = 250"],"a":"250"},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",25]],"s":["I_C = βI_B = 100 × 100 μA = 10 mA.","I_E = I_B + I_C = 0.1 + 10 = 10.1 mA.","I_C/I_E = 10/10.1 = 0.99 (this is α)."],"a":"I_C = 10 mA, I_E = 10.1 mA, I_C/I_E = 0.99"},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",26]],"s":["I_C = V/R_C = 6/3000 = 2 × 10⁻³ A.","β = α/(1 − α) = 0.97/0.03 = 32.3.","I_B = I_C/β = 2 × 10⁻³/32.3 = 6.2 × 10⁻⁵ A (62 μA).","(For comparison, I_E = I_C/α = 2.06 × 10⁻³ A.)"],"a":"I_B ≈ 6.2 × 10⁻⁵ A","n":"The printed answer 2.06 × 10⁻³ A is the emitter current I_E; the base current asked for is about 62 μA."},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",27]],"s":["α = I_C/I_E, so I_E = I_C/α.","I_E = 60/0.96","I_E = 62.5 μA"],"a":"62.5 μA"},{"q":[["phys17-exercise-mcq9-12-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical-252-253",28]],"s":["I_E = I_B + I_C, so I_B = I_E − I_C.","I_B = 3.5 − 2.35","I_B = 1.15 A"],"a":"1.15 A"}]}},"18":{"items":{"mcqs":[{"q":[["phys18-unit-key-points-and-exercise-mcq-292-293",19]],"s":["A and B travel the same distance at the same speed, so the same time dilation applies to both: they age equally.","Moving clocks run slow, so both are younger than C who stayed on Earth: A = B < C."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys18-unit-key-points-and-exercise-mcq-292-293",20]],"s":["Proper time is measured by a clock present at both events (departure and arrival).","Only the observer on the spaceship is at both events, so he measures the proper time."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys18-unit-key-points-and-exercise-mcq-292-293",21]],"s":["Compressing the spring stores elastic potential energy ΔE in it.","By E = mc² its mass increases by Δm = ΔE/c² (far too small to measure)."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys18-unit-key-points-and-exercise-mcq-292-293",22]],"s":["Stefan–Boltzmann law: E ∝ T⁴.","Doubling T multiplies E by 2⁴ = 16."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys18-unit-key-points-and-exercise-mcq-292-293",23]],"s":["Colour fixes the frequency and hence the energy hf of each photon.","Greater brightness (intensity) at the same colour means more photons per second."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys18-unit-key-points-and-exercise-mcq-292-293",24]],"s":["Number of photons per second N = P/(hf) = Pλ/(hc).","For the same power, the light with the longest wavelength (lowest photon energy) emits the most photons: red."],"n":"A stray marker '[7]' printed inside choice C has been removed.","inline":1,"r":[["[7] ",""]],"c":0},{"q":[["phys18-unit-key-points-and-exercise-mcq-292-293",25]],"s":["Photoemission needs photon energy ≥ work function, 3.00 eV.","1.50 eV and 2.90 eV are too small; the least energy that can produce photoelectrons is 3.50 eV."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",0]],"s":["The electron gains energy K = E − E′, which is largest when the photon loses most energy.","Δλ = (h/mc)(1 − cos θ) is maximum when the photon is scattered back at θ = 180°; the electron then recoils straight forward with maximum energy."],"n":"The angle refers to the photon's scattering angle (180°); the electron itself moves forward along the original direction.","inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",1]],"s":["Compton shift Δλ = (h/mc)(1 − cos θ) is inversely proportional to the mass of the scatterer.","A proton is about 1836 times heavier than an electron, so the shift decreases."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",2]],"s":["A single photon in vacuum cannot change into an electron–positron pair: energy and momentum cannot both be conserved.","A heavy nucleus nearby is needed to take up the extra momentum. So it cannot occur because momentum would not be conserved."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",3]],"s":["λ = h/p = h/(mv). For particles with the same speed (or the same kinetic energy, λ = h/√(2mK)), the heaviest particle has the smallest wavelength.","The α-particle is the heaviest, so its wavelength is smallest."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",4]],"s":["Same λ means same momentum p = h/λ.","K = p²/(2m): the electron, with much smaller mass, has the greater kinetic energy."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",5]],"s":["An electron microscope uses a beam of fast electrons, whose very short de Broglie wavelength gives a much higher resolution than light."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",6]],"s":["Δx Δp ≥ h/(4π), so Δx ∝ 1/Δp.","Reducing Δp by a factor of 2 increases Δx by a factor of 2."],"inline":1,"c":2}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",8]],"s":["Everyday speeds (cars, trains at 20–40 m/s) would then be close to the speed of light, so relativistic effects would be obvious.","Moving vehicles would look shorter (length contraction), clocks in them would run slow (time dilation) and their mass would increase; nothing could move faster than 50 m/s.","We would also see events noticeably late, and moving objects would show strong Doppler colour changes."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",9]],"s":["By Wien's displacement law λ_max T = constant (2.9 × 10⁻³ m K).","Blue stars emit most strongly at shorter wavelengths, so they have the higher surface temperature; reddish stars are cooler."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",10]],"s":["No. Photoemission occurs only if the photon energy hf is greater than the work function of the metal.","Different metals have different work functions (threshold frequencies), so light that ejects electrons from one metal (e.g. zinc in UV) may not eject any from another (e.g. platinum)."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",11]],"s":["No. By Wien's law a filament at 2500 K has λ_max = 2.9 × 10⁻³/2500 ≈ 1.2 μm, in the infrared; most of its radiation is infrared and its visible light is yellowish-red.","The Sun at 6000 K has λ_max ≈ 480 nm, in the middle of the visible spectrum, so its light is much whiter."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",12]],"s":["Energy of one photon E = hc/λ. Red light has a longer wavelength, so each red photon carries less energy.","For the same total energy, the red beam contains the greater number of photons."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",13]],"s":["A photon always travels at the speed of light c.","Relativistic mass m = m₀/√(1 − v²/c²) would be infinite at v = c unless the rest mass m₀ = 0.","So the rest mass of a photon must be zero; its energy is entirely kinetic, E = pc = hf."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",14]],"s":["The maximum Compton shift is 2h/(m_ec) ≈ 4.9 × 10⁻¹² m.","Visible light has λ ≈ 5 × 10⁻⁷ m, so the fractional change (≈ 10⁻⁵) is far too small to detect.","Moreover, visible photons (≈ 2–3 eV) are comparable to electron binding energies, so the electrons do not behave as free particles; Compton scattering is noticeable only for X-rays and γ-rays."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",15]],"s":["Same de Broglie wavelength means the same momentum: m_ev_e = m_pv_p.","The electron has much smaller mass, so it has the greater speed (about 1836 times that of the proton)."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",16]],"s":["Both gain the same kinetic energy K = eV.","λ = h/p = h/√(2mK). The electron has much smaller mass, so smaller momentum.","Hence the electron has the longer de Broglie wavelength."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",17]],"s":["Ultraviolet photons have higher frequency and energy (about 3–12 eV) than visible photons (about 1.8–3 eV).","UV photons can break chemical bonds and ionise molecules, damaging the DNA and proteins in skin cells — causing sunburn, ageing and skin cancer. Visible photons do not carry enough energy to do this."]}],"longQuestions":[{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",19]],"s":["A frame of reference is a coordinate system with respect to which positions and motions are measured.","Inertial frame: a frame that is at rest or moves with uniform velocity (not accelerated); Newton's laws hold in it. Examples: a train moving smoothly at constant velocity, the Earth for most everyday purposes, a spacecraft drifting freely.","Non-inertial frame: an accelerated (or rotating) frame; Newton's laws do not hold without introducing fictitious forces. Examples: a car braking or turning (passengers are thrown forward or outward), a lift accelerating upward, a merry-go-round.","Special relativity deals with inertial frames; all inertial frames are equivalent."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",20]],"s":["Postulate 1 (principle of relativity): the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames.","Postulate 2: the speed of light in vacuum is the same (c) for all inertial observers, regardless of the motion of the source or observer.","Results: (i) Time dilation — a moving clock runs slow: t = t₀/√(1 − v²/c²). (ii) Length contraction — a moving length shrinks along the motion: L = L₀√(1 − v²/c²).","(iii) Mass increase: m = m₀/√(1 − v²/c²). (iv) Mass–energy equivalence: E = mc²; rest energy m₀c². (v) Relativity of simultaneity; no material body can reach the speed of light.","These results are confirmed by muon decay, particle accelerators and GPS clocks."]},{"q":[["phys18-exercise-mcq-8-14-conceptual-comprehensive-294-295",21]],"s":["A black body absorbs all radiation falling on it and is also the best emitter. Its radiation depends only on its temperature.","Features: the intensity–wavelength curve is continuous with a peak; the peak shifts to shorter wavelengths as temperature rises (Wien's law λ_maxT = 2.9 × 10⁻³ m K); the total energy radiated per second per unit area rises as T⁴ (Stefan's law E = σT⁴).","Classical theory (Rayleigh–Jeans) predicted infinite energy at short wavelengths — the 'ultraviolet catastrophe'.","Planck's quantum theory: energy is emitted or absorbed not continuously but in discrete packets (quanta) of energy E = hf, h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s. His formula fitted the experimental curves exactly.","Importance: it began quantum physics — leading to Einstein's photon theory, Bohr's atom and quantum mechanics."]},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",0]],"s":["Photoelectric effect: emission of electrons from a metal surface when light of suitable frequency falls on it.","Observations: no emission below a threshold frequency, however intense the light; the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons depends on frequency, not intensity; the number of electrons (current) depends on intensity; emission is instantaneous.","Failure of classical (wave) theory: it predicted emission at any frequency with enough intensity, K.E. increasing with intensity, and a time delay at low intensity — all contrary to experiment.","Photon explanation (Einstein): light consists of photons of energy hf. One photon gives all its energy to one electron: hf = φ + K_max, where φ = hf₀ is the work function.","This explains the threshold frequency (hf < φ gives no emission), K_max increasing linearly with f, current ∝ number of photons (intensity), and instant emission."]},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",1]],"s":["Compton effect: when X-rays are scattered by loosely bound (free) electrons, the scattered X-rays have a longer wavelength than the incident ones, depending on the scattering angle.","Treat the X-ray as a photon of energy hf and momentum h/λ colliding elastically with an electron at rest.","Energy conservation: hc/λ + m₀c² = hc/λ′ + mc². Momentum conservation along and perpendicular to the incident direction: h/λ = (h/λ′) cos θ + p cos φ; 0 = (h/λ′) sin θ − p sin φ.","Eliminating the electron's recoil angle φ and momentum p (using E² = p²c² + m₀²c⁴) gives:","Δλ = λ′ − λ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos θ). h/m₀c = 2.43 × 10⁻¹² m is the Compton wavelength. Δλ is zero at θ = 0 and maximum (2h/m₀c) at θ = 180°. This confirmed the particle (photon) nature of light."]},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",2]],"s":["Pair production: a high-energy γ-ray photon passing near a heavy nucleus disappears and an electron–positron pair is created: γ → e⁻ + e⁺.","Minimum photon energy = 2m₀c² = 1.02 MeV; any extra energy becomes the kinetic energy of the pair: hf = 2m₀c² + K₋ + K₊. The nucleus is needed to conserve momentum; charge is conserved (−e + e = 0).","Annihilation: when a positron meets an electron, both disappear and their mass is converted into energy — usually two γ-photons of 0.51 MeV each, moving in opposite directions (to conserve momentum).","These processes show the conversion of energy into matter and matter into energy, confirming E = mc². Annihilation is used in PET scanning."]},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",3]],"s":["De Broglie hypothesis: just as light has particle properties, moving material particles have wave properties. The wavelength of a particle of momentum p is λ = h/p = h/(mv).","Davisson–Germer experiment (or G. P. Thomson's): a beam of electrons accelerated through a known p.d. was directed at a nickel crystal (or a thin metal foil).","The scattered electrons showed intensity maxima at particular angles — a diffraction pattern, like X-rays diffracted by a crystal (or rings like light through a fine grating).","The wavelength found from the diffraction (Bragg's law) agreed with λ = h/p calculated from the accelerating voltage (e.g. 54 V gives 0.167 nm).","Diffraction is a wave property, so this proved that electrons have wave nature. It is the basis of the electron microscope."]},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",4]],"s":["Wave–particle duality: both light and matter show wave properties in some experiments and particle properties in others.","Light: interference, diffraction and polarisation show wave nature; the photoelectric effect, Compton effect and pair production show particle (photon) nature.","Matter: electrons, neutrons and atoms behave as particles (definite mass, charge, collisions) but also diffract like waves (Davisson–Germer), with λ = h/p.","The two aspects never appear together in one experiment; which one is seen depends on the experiment (Bohr's principle of complementarity). The link is E = hf and p = h/λ."]},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",5]],"s":["Heisenberg's uncertainty principle: it is impossible to measure simultaneously both the position and the momentum of a particle with unlimited accuracy: Δx Δp ≥ h/(4π). Similarly ΔE Δt ≥ h/(4π).","It is not due to imperfect instruments; it is a fundamental consequence of the wave nature of matter.","Thought experiment (gamma-ray microscope): to locate an electron we must illuminate it. The position uncertainty is about the wavelength used: Δx ≈ λ.","The photon that reveals the electron gives it a recoil momentum of about h/λ, which is unknown: Δp ≈ h/λ.","So Δx Δp ≈ h. Using shorter λ improves Δx but increases Δp, and vice versa — the product can never be reduced below the order of h."]}],"numericals":[{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",7]],"s":["L = L₀√(1 − v²/c²) with L = L₀/3.","√(1 − v²/c²) = 1/3, so 1 − v²/c² = 1/9 and v²/c² = 8/9.","v = c√(8/9) = 0.9428c"],"a":"0.9428c"},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",8]],"s":["t = t₀/√(1 − v²/c²) = 3/√(1 − 0.95²)","√(1 − 0.9025) = √0.0975 = 0.312","t = 3/0.312 = 9.6 s"],"a":"9.6 s"},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",9]],"s":["(a) f₀ = φ/h = (3.0 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴) = 0.72 × 10¹⁵ Hz.","Photon energy E = hc/λ = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸)/(3 × 10⁻⁷) = 6.63 × 10⁻¹⁹ J = 4.14 eV.","(b) K_max = E − φ = 4.14 − 3.0 = 1.14 eV (≈ 1.16 eV in the book).","(c) Stopping potential V₀ = K_max/e = 1.14 V."],"a":"(a) 7.2 × 10¹⁴ Hz (b) ≈ 1.14 eV (c) ≈ 1.14 V","n":"With h = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ J s and c = 3 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ the energy is 1.14 eV; the printed 1.16 eV comes from rounding the constants."},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",10]],"s":["λ = hc/E = 1240 eV nm/50 000 eV = 0.0248 nm = 24.8 pm.","Δλ = (h/m₀c)(1 − cos 90°) = 2.43 pm.","λ′ = 24.8 + 2.43 = 27.2 pm.","E′ = hc/λ′ = 1240/0.0272 eV = 45.5 keV."],"a":"45.5 keV"},{"q":[["phys18-unit-review-comprehensive-4-9-numericals-1-5-296",11]],"s":["Δv = 0.003% of 5 × 10³ = 0.00003 × 5000 = 0.15 m s⁻¹.","Δp = mΔv = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ × 0.15 = 1.37 × 10⁻³¹ kg m s⁻¹.","Using Δx Δp ≈ h (as in the book): Δx = 6.63 × 10⁻³⁴/1.37 × 10⁻³¹ = 4.84 × 10⁻³ m."],"a":"≈ 4.8 × 10⁻³ m","n":"The book uses the order-of-magnitude form Δx Δp ≈ h. With the exact form Δx Δp ≥ h/4π the minimum uncertainty is about 3.9 × 10⁻⁴ m."}]}},"19":{"items":{"mcqs":[{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",1]],"s":["Lyman series (to n = 1) is ultraviolet; Paschen and Brackett (to n = 3, 4) are infrared.","The Balmer series (to n = 2) lies in the visible region."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",2]],"s":["The longest wavelength of a series comes from the smallest energy jump.","For Balmer (ending at n = 2) that is the transition from n = 3: λ = 656.2 nm (H-α)."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",3]],"s":["E_n = −13.6/n² eV; for n = 2, E₂ = −13.6/4 = −3.4 eV.","Energy needed to remove the electron from n = 2 is 3.4 eV."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",4]],"s":["From level n the possible transitions to all lower levels number n(n − 1)/2.","For n = 5: 5 × 4/2 = 10."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",5]],"s":["E_n = −13.6/n² eV.","E₄ = −13.6/16 = −0.85 eV."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",6]],"s":["r_n = n²r₁.","r₃ = 9r₁, a factor of 9."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",7]],"s":["X-ray production: kinetic energy of electrons → photon energy.","The photoelectric effect is the reverse: photon energy → kinetic energy of an electron."],"inline":1,"c":1},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",8]],"s":["Ordinary excited states last about 10⁻⁸ s, but a metastable state lasts much longer, about 10⁻³ s.","This long lifetime makes population inversion possible."],"inline":1,"c":0},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",9]],"s":["The He-Ne laser emits at 632.8 nm, which is red light."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",10]],"s":["The He-Ne laser tube is filled with about 85% helium and 15% neon (at low pressure)."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",11]],"s":["Photon energy = difference between the levels.","E = 20.66 − 18.70 = 1.96 eV (632.8 nm, red)."],"inline":1,"c":3},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",12]],"s":["L-series X-rays are emitted when electrons fall into the L shell (n = 2).","L_α is from n = 3 and L_β from n = 4: n = 4 → n = 2."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",13]],"s":["Pumping supplies energy to raise atoms to higher (metastable) levels.","Its purpose is to achieve population inversion — more atoms in the upper level than in the lower level."],"inline":1,"c":2},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",14]],"s":["In Bohr's model mvr = nh/2π and r ∝ n², so v ∝ 1/n.","Momentum p = mv ∝ 1/n."],"inline":1,"c":1}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",16]],"s":["A hydrogen sample contains an enormous number of atoms. Different atoms are excited to different energy levels.","Each electron can return to lower levels by many different possible jumps (e.g. 3→2, 3→1, 4→2…). Each kind of jump emits a photon of a particular energy, giving one line. So the spectrum has many lines even though each atom has one electron."]},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",17]],"s":["In a solid the atoms are packed closely and interact strongly, so their energy levels broaden into continuous bands; electrons can make transitions of any energy and all wavelengths are emitted — a continuous spectrum (thermal radiation).","In a hot, low-density gas the atoms are far apart and isolated; each has sharp, discrete energy levels, so only definite wavelengths are emitted — a line spectrum."]},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",18]],"s":["(a) A photon of less than 13.6 eV is absorbed only if its energy exactly equals the difference between the ground state and an excited state (10.2 eV, 12.09 eV, 12.75 eV, …). Otherwise it is not absorbed.","(b) Yes. Any photon with energy greater than 13.6 eV can be absorbed: it ionises the atom and the extra energy appears as kinetic energy of the freed electron."]},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",19]],"s":["E_n = −13.6/n² eV: as n increases the levels get closer together, crowding towards the ionisation limit (E = 0).","So the energy differences, and hence the wavelengths, of successive lines in a series differ less and less, and the lines converge towards the series limit."]},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",20]],"s":["By classical electromagnetism an orbiting (accelerating) electron should radiate energy continuously, spiral into the nucleus and give a continuous spectrum — atoms would be unstable.","Bohr postulated that only orbits with angular momentum mvr = nh/2π are allowed, in which the electron does not radiate. This quantisation gives stable orbits with definite energies, explaining the stability of atoms and the line spectrum."]},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",21]],"s":["Light comes from transitions of the outer (valence) electrons, involving energy differences of a few eV.","X-rays come from transitions of inner-shell electrons in heavy atoms (after an inner electron is knocked out), involving energies of thousands of eV.","So X-ray photons have much higher energy and shorter wavelength; they are penetrating and ionising, unlike visible light."]},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",22]],"s":["(a) Bremsstrahlung (continuous) X-rays: the shortest wavelength λ_min = hc/(eV) decreases, so the continuous spectrum extends to shorter wavelengths (and becomes more intense).","(b) Characteristic X-rays: their wavelengths do not change, because they depend only on the energy levels of the target element; only their intensity increases (and new lines may appear once V exceeds the needed excitation energy)."]},{"q":[["phys19-exercise-mcq-conceptual-330-331",23]],"s":["Diffraction is noticeable only when the wavelength is comparable to the spacing of the 'grating'.","The spacing between atoms in a crystal is about 0.1 nm, comparable to X-ray wavelengths, so crystals diffract X-rays. UV wavelengths (≈ 10–400 nm) are far too long, so no useful diffraction pattern is formed."]}],"longQuestions":[{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",1]],"s":["When hydrogen gas is excited in a discharge tube, it emits a line spectrum: definite wavelengths arranged in series.","All lines fit the formula 1/λ = R_H(1/p² − 1/n²), with R_H = 1.097 × 10⁷ m⁻¹ (Rydberg constant), p = 1, 2, 3… and n = p + 1, p + 2, …","Lyman series (p = 1): ultraviolet. Balmer series (p = 2): visible (H-α 656 nm, H-β 486 nm, H-γ 434 nm, H-δ 410 nm). Paschen (p = 3), Brackett (p = 4) and Pfund (p = 5): infrared.","In each series the lines get closer together towards the series limit (n → ∞).","Bohr's theory explains this: each line is a photon emitted when the electron jumps from level n to level p, hf = E_n − E_p."]},{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",2]],"s":["Bohr's postulates: (1) The electron revolves in certain circular orbits without radiating energy (stationary states). (2) In these orbits the angular momentum is quantised: mvr = nh/2π. (3) Radiation is emitted or absorbed only when the electron jumps between orbits: hf = E_n − E_p.","(a) Radius: the electrostatic force provides the centripetal force: ke²/r² = mv²/r. With v = nh/(2πmr): r_n = n²h²/(4π²mke²) = n² × 0.053 nm.","(b) Energy: K.E. = ½mv² = ke²/(2r), P.E. = −ke²/r, so total E = −ke²/(2r).","Substituting r_n: E_n = −2π²mk²e⁴/(n²h²) = −13.6/n² eV.","The negative energy means the electron is bound; E₁ = −13.6 eV is the ground state."]},{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",3]],"s":["Ground state: the lowest energy state of an atom (n = 1 for hydrogen, E₁ = −13.6 eV), in which it is most stable.","Excited state: any higher energy state (n = 2, 3, …) reached when the atom absorbs energy; the atom stays there only about 10⁻⁸ s before returning.","Excitation energy: the energy needed to raise an electron from the ground state to an excited state, e.g. 10.2 eV for n = 1 → 2 in hydrogen (the corresponding voltage is the excitation potential).","Ionisation energy: the minimum energy needed to remove the electron completely from the ground state to infinity (E = 0); 13.6 eV for hydrogen (ionisation potential 13.6 V)."]},{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",4]],"s":["X-rays are electromagnetic waves of very short wavelength (about 0.01–10 nm), discovered by Roentgen.","Production: in an X-ray (Coolidge) tube, electrons from a heated filament are accelerated by a high p.d. (tens of kV) and strike a heavy-metal target (tungsten); about 1% of their energy becomes X-rays, the rest heat.","Continuous X-rays (bremsstrahlung): produced when electrons are decelerated by the nuclei of the target; all wavelengths down to λ_min = hc/(eV) occur; λ_min depends on the tube voltage, not the target.","Characteristic X-rays: an incoming electron knocks out an inner-shell (K or L) electron; an outer electron falls into the vacancy, emitting a photon of definite energy (K_α, K_β, L_α…). These sharp lines are characteristic of the target element and do not depend on the voltage."]},{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",5]],"s":["LASER: Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation — an intense, monochromatic, coherent and highly directional beam.","Principle: stimulated emission. An incoming photon of energy hf = E₂ − E₁ causes an excited atom to emit an identical photon (same frequency, phase and direction), so light is amplified.","Requirements: population inversion (more atoms in the upper level than the lower), produced by pumping into a metastable state (lifetime ≈ 10⁻³ s), and an optical resonator (two parallel mirrors, one partly silvered) to reflect photons back and forth.","He-Ne laser operation: an electric discharge excites He atoms to a metastable level; collisions transfer the energy to Ne atoms, giving population inversion in Ne. Stimulated transitions in Ne emit 632.8 nm red light, which builds up between the mirrors and emerges through the partly silvered mirror.","Uses: surgery, eye operations, CD/DVD players, fibre-optic communication, holography, cutting and welding."]}],"numericals":[{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",7],["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",8]],"s":["Paschen series: 1/λ = R_H(1/3² − 1/n²), n = 4, 5, …","Longest λ (n = 4): 1/λ = 1.097 × 10⁷(1/9 − 1/16) = 1.097 × 10⁷ × 7/144, so λ = 1.875 × 10⁻⁶ m = 1875 nm.","Shortest λ (n = ∞): 1/λ = R_H/9, so λ = 9/1.097 × 10⁷ = 8.20 × 10⁻⁷ m = 820 nm."],"a":"Longest 1875 nm; shortest 820 nm"},{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",9],["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",10]],"s":["r_n = n²r₁ = 25 × 0.0529 nm = 1.32 nm.","E_n = −13.6/n² eV = −13.6/25 = −0.544 eV.","In joules: −0.544 × 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = −8.7 × 10⁻²⁰ J."],"a":"r₅ = 1.32 nm; E₅ = −0.544 eV = −8.7 × 10⁻²⁰ J"},{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",11],["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",12]],"s":["(a) E = E₂ − E₁ = −3.4 − (−13.6) = 10.2 eV = 1.63 × 10⁻¹⁸ J.","(b) f = E/h = 1.63 × 10⁻¹⁸/6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ = 2.46 × 10¹⁵ Hz.","(c) λ = c/f = 3 × 10⁸/2.46 × 10¹⁵ = 1.22 × 10⁻⁷ m ≈ 122 nm."],"a":"(a) 10.2 eV (b) 2.46 × 10¹⁵ Hz (c) ≈ 122 nm","n":"The book rounds the wavelength to 120 nm."},{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",13],["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",14]],"s":["Bohr's condition: 2πr_n = nλ, so λ = 2πr_n/n.","r₄ = 16 × 0.0529 nm = 0.846 nm.","λ = 2π × 0.846/4 = 1.33 nm."],"a":"1.33 nm"},{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",15],["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",16]],"s":["(a) v₁ = ke²/(ħ) = 2πke²/h = (2π × 9 × 10⁹ × (1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹)²)/(6.63 × 10⁻³⁴) = 2.19 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹.","(b) T = 2πr₁/v₁ = (2π × 0.529 × 10⁻¹⁰)/(2.19 × 10⁶) = 1.52 × 10⁻¹⁶ s."],"a":"(a) 2.19 × 10⁶ m s⁻¹ (b) 1.52 × 10⁻¹⁶ s"},{"q":[["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",17],["phys19-comprehensive-numerical-exercises-332",18]],"s":["λ_min = hc/(eV), so V = hc/(eλ_min).","V = (6.63 × 10⁻³⁴ × 3 × 10⁸)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.46 × 10⁻⁹)","V = 2.7 × 10³ V = 2.7 kV"],"a":"2.7 kV"}]}},"20":{"items":{"mcqs":[{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",21]],"s":["1 u = 1.660 × 10⁻²⁷ kg.","Its energy equivalent is E = mc² = 1.4924 × 10⁻¹⁰ J = 931.5 MeV. All three are correct."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",22],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",23],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",24],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",25]],"c":3},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",26]],"s":["⁷Li has mass number A = 7 and atomic number Z = 3.","Neutrons N = A − Z = 7 − 3 = 4."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",27],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",28],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",29],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",30]],"c":1},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",31]],"s":["When nucleons combine, some mass is converted into binding energy (mass defect).","So the sum of the masses of the separate nucleons is greater than the mass of the nucleus."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",32],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",33],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",34],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",35]],"c":1},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",36]],"s":["β⁻ particles are fast electrons emitted from the nucleus (n → p + e⁻ + ν̄).","So β⁻ radiation is similar to electrons."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",37],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",38],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",39],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",40]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",41]],"s":["1 curie = 3.70 × 10¹⁰ disintegrations per second.","1 Ci = 3.70 × 10¹⁰ Bq."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",42],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",43],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",44],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",45]],"c":3},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",46]],"s":["After n half-lives the remaining amount is m₀/2ⁿ.","20 mg/2⁴ = 20/16 = 1.25 mg."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",47],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",48],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",49],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",50]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",51]],"s":["From N = N₀e^(−λt), λt is dimensionless.","So λ has unit s⁻¹."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",52],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",53],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",54],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",55]],"c":3},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",56]],"s":["²³⁵U is fissioned most efficiently by slow (thermal) neutrons.","Thermal neutrons have energies of about 0.04 eV (≈ 0.025 eV at room temperature)."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",57],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",58],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",59],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",60]],"c":3},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",61]],"s":["Balance mass numbers: 1 + 16 = A + 2, so A = 15.","Balance charges: 0 + 8 = Z + 1, so Z = 7. The nucleus is ¹⁵₇N."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",62],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",63],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",64],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",65]],"c":0},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",66]],"s":["A γ-ray carries no charge and no nucleons. In γ + X → Y + n the target X has the same Z as the product Y and one more nucleon.","The product nuclide is unreadable in the source. If it is ¹³⁷₅₆Ba (the usual form of this problem), the target is ¹³⁸₅₆Ba — option A."],"n":"The product nuclide in this reaction is illegible in the source scan, so the answer is conditional: the target has the product's Z and one more nucleon (A for a ¹³⁷₅₆Ba product).","o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",67],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",68],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",69],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",70]]},{"q":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",71]],"s":["Natural uranium is about 99.3% ²³⁸U and only about 0.7% ²³⁵U."],"o":[["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",72],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",73],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",74],["phys20-glossary-and-exercise-mcq-378-379",75]],"c":0},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",0]],"s":["A fast breeder reactor uses the fertile isotope ²³⁸U (with ²³⁹Pu as fuel).","Fast neutrons convert ²³⁸U into fissile ²³⁹Pu, breeding more fuel than is used."],"o":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",1],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",2],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",3],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",4]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",5]],"s":["The strong nuclear force is charge-independent.","It acts equally between proton–proton, proton–neutron and neutron–neutron pairs."],"o":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",6],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",7],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",8],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",9]],"c":3},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",10]],"s":["Bosons have integer spin; fermions (protons, neutrinos) have half-integer spin.","The photon (spin 1) is a boson. The pion (spin 0) is also a boson."],"n":"Both the photon (C) and the pion (D) are bosons; the photon is the intended answer as the force-carrying gauge boson.","o":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",11],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",12],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",13],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",14]],"c":2},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",15]],"s":["Hadrons made of a quark and an antiquark (q q̄) are mesons; baryons are made of three quarks."],"o":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",16],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",17],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",18],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",19]],"c":1},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",20]],"s":["Quarks are never found free; they combine so that the total colour charge is neutral ('white').","All free particles have zero colour charge."],"o":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",21],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",22],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",23],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",24]],"c":0}],"shortQuestions":[{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",26]],"s":["The electrostatic repulsion between protons increases rapidly with Z (it acts between every pair of protons), while the strong nuclear force is short-ranged and acts only between neighbours.","Extra neutrons add attractive strong force without adding repulsion, holding the nucleus together. So heavy nuclei need more neutrons than protons (N/Z ≈ 1.5 for heavy nuclei)."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",27]],"s":["Radium is continuously produced in the uranium-238 decay series.","²³⁸U has a half-life of about 4.5 × 10⁹ years, so it is still plentiful, and its decay chain keeps forming new radium. So radium is found in uranium ores even though its own half-life is only 1600 years."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",28]],"s":["No. Radioactive decay is a random (spontaneous) process: we can only state the probability per unit time (the decay constant λ) that a nucleus will decay.","The half-life is a statistical quantity that applies to a large number of nuclei; the moment a particular nucleus decays cannot be predicted."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",29]],"s":["Neutrons have no charge, so they feel no electrostatic repulsion from the nucleus.","Even slow neutrons can approach and enter a nucleus easily; slow neutrons stay near the nucleus longer and are captured readily, causing nuclear reactions such as fission."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",30]],"s":["When a nucleus absorbs a neutron it becomes neutron-rich (too many neutrons for its number of protons).","To approach the stability line it must convert a neutron into a proton: n → p + e⁻ + ν̄, which is β⁻ decay. β⁺ decay (p → n) would make it even more neutron-rich."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",31]],"s":["Stability depends on the binding energy per nucleon, which is maximum (≈ 8.8 MeV) near iron-56.","Very light nuclei have few nucleons, so many nucleons are at the surface and the binding energy per nucleon is low; they can become more stable by fusing.","Very heavy nuclei have large proton–proton repulsion that weakens the binding; they become more stable by α-decay or fission."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",32]],"s":["Reactor fuel is only slightly enriched (about 3–4% ²³⁵U), while a bomb needs highly enriched material (≈ 90%) assembled very rapidly into a supercritical mass.","In a reactor the chain reaction is kept just critical (k = 1) by control rods and the moderator, and it relies on delayed neutrons, so it changes slowly and can be controlled.","If it overheats, the moderator is lost or expands and the reaction slows. A reactor can overheat or melt down, but it cannot explode like a nuclear bomb."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",33]],"s":["The binding energy per nucleon of light nuclei increases with mass number (e.g. ²H 1.1 MeV, ⁴He 7.1 MeV).","When light nuclei fuse, the product is more tightly bound: its mass is less than the total mass of the reacting nuclei.","The mass defect Δm is released as energy E = Δmc² (e.g. ²H + ³H → ⁴He + n + 17.6 MeV)."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",34]],"s":["Both nuclei are positively charged, so a very large Coulomb barrier repels them; they must have huge kinetic energies to come within range of the strong force.","This needs temperatures of about 10⁷–10⁸ K, at which matter is a plasma, together with high density and long enough confinement time (Lawson criterion).","No material container can hold such hot plasma; it must be confined by strong magnetic fields (tokamak) or by lasers (inertial confinement), which is technically very difficult."]}],"longQuestions":[{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",36]],"s":["Natural radioactivity is the spontaneous emission of radiation by unstable nuclei of heavy elements (Z > 82), such as uranium, thorium and radium, without any external cause. It was discovered by Becquerel in 1896.","The rate of emission is unaffected by temperature, pressure or chemical combination; it is a nuclear property.","Classification (by passing the radiation through a magnetic or electric field): α-rays bend slightly one way — they are positively charged helium nuclei (⁴₂He); β-rays bend strongly the other way — they are fast electrons; γ-rays are undeflected — they are uncharged high-energy electromagnetic photons.","Penetration: α stopped by paper, β by a few mm of aluminium, γ needs several cm of lead. Ionising power is greatest for α and least for γ."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",37]],"s":["A mass spectrometer separates ions according to their mass and measures their masses (it shows the existence of isotopes).","Construction: an ion source (ions of charge q accelerated by p.d. V), a velocity selector (crossed E and B fields), and a deflection chamber with a uniform magnetic field B, plus a detector/photographic plate.","Velocity selector: only ions with qE = qvB pass straight through, so all have speed v = E/B.","In the deflection chamber the magnetic force provides the centripetal force: qvB = mv²/r, so r = mv/(qB) and m = qBr/v = qB r B/E.","Ions of different mass (isotopes) travel on circles of different radii and strike the plate at different places; the positions give the masses and the intensities give the relative abundances."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",38]],"s":["Isotopes are atoms of the same element having the same atomic number Z (same number of protons and electrons) but different mass numbers A (different numbers of neutrons).","They have the same chemical properties but different physical (nuclear) properties.","Examples: hydrogen — ¹H (protium), ²H (deuterium), ³H (tritium); carbon — ¹²C, ¹³C, ¹⁴C (radioactive); uranium — ²³⁵U and ²³⁸U; chlorine — ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl.","Isotopes are separated and identified with a mass spectrometer."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",39]],"s":["Mass defect: the mass of a nucleus is always less than the total mass of its separate protons and neutrons. Δm = Zm_p + (A − Z)m_n − m_nucleus.","Binding energy: the energy equivalent of the mass defect, E_b = Δmc² (1 u = 931.5 MeV); it is the energy needed to separate the nucleus into its nucleons, or released when they combine.","Example: ⁴He: Δm = 0.0304 u, E_b = 28.3 MeV.","Binding energy per nucleon E_b/A measures stability; its graph against A rises steeply for light nuclei, peaks near ⁵⁶Fe (≈ 8.8 MeV) and falls slowly for heavy nuclei — explaining why fusion of light nuclei and fission of heavy nuclei release energy."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",40]],"s":["Half-life T½ is the time in which half of the radioactive nuclei present in a sample decay.","Decay law: N = N₀e^(−λt). Putting N = N₀/2 at t = T½ gives e^(−λT½) = ½, so T½ = ln 2/λ = 0.693/λ.","After n half-lives the remaining number is N₀/2ⁿ (½, ¼, ⅛ …).","Half-life is characteristic of each radioisotope and ranges from fractions of a second to billions of years (e.g. ¹³¹I 8 days, ¹⁴C 5730 years, ²³⁸U 4.5 × 10⁹ years). It is unaffected by physical or chemical conditions."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",41]],"s":["A nuclear reaction occurs when a nucleus is struck by a particle (proton, neutron, α-particle, deuteron, photon) and changes into a different nucleus, e.g. ¹⁴₇N + ⁴₂He → ¹⁷₈O + ¹₁H (Rutherford, 1919).","In every nuclear reaction the total charge (Z) and the total number of nucleons (A) are conserved, as are energy and momentum.","Q-value: Q = (mass of reactants − mass of products)c². If Q > 0, energy is released (exoergic); if Q < 0, energy must be supplied (endoergic).","Examples: neutron capture, transmutation, fission and fusion."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",42]],"s":["Nuclear fission is the splitting of a heavy nucleus into two medium-sized nuclei when it absorbs a neutron, with release of energy and more neutrons.","Example: ¹n + ²³⁵U → ²³⁶U* → ¹⁴¹Ba + ⁹²Kr + 3¹n + about 200 MeV.","Energy: the products have higher binding energy per nucleon, so mass is lost; the mass defect appears mostly as the kinetic energy of the fragments (heat).","Chain reaction: the 2–3 neutrons released can fission further nuclei. If more than one neutron per fission causes further fission, the reaction grows rapidly (bomb); if exactly one does, it is steady (reactor). A minimum (critical) mass is needed.","Fission was discovered by Hahn and Strassmann (1938); it is used in nuclear power stations and atomic bombs."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",43]],"s":["A nuclear reactor is a device in which a controlled, self-sustaining fission chain reaction produces heat (used to generate electricity).","Principle: controlled chain reaction with on average one neutron from each fission causing one further fission (k = 1).","Construction: fuel rods (enriched uranium); moderator (water, heavy water or graphite) to slow neutrons; control rods (cadmium or boron) to absorb neutrons; coolant (water or CO₂) to remove heat; thick concrete shielding.","Working: heat from fission is carried by the coolant to a heat exchanger, producing steam that drives turbines and generators. Raising or lowering the control rods adjusts the rate of reaction.","Uses: generating electricity, producing radioisotopes for medicine and industry, research, and propulsion of submarines and ships."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",44]],"s":["Nuclear fusion is the combining of two light nuclei to form a heavier nucleus with release of energy.","Energy release: the product is more tightly bound (higher binding energy per nucleon), so its mass is less than that of the reactants; the mass defect is released as E = Δmc².","Examples: ²₁H + ³₁H → ⁴₂He + ¹₀n + 17.6 MeV; in the Sun the proton–proton chain converts four protons into one ⁴He nucleus releasing about 26.7 MeV.","Fusion needs temperatures of about 10⁷ K or more to overcome the Coulomb repulsion (thermonuclear reactions). It powers the Sun and stars and the hydrogen bomb; controlled fusion for electricity is under research (tokamaks)."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",45]],"s":["A radiation detector is a device that detects and counts nuclear radiation by the ionisation or excitation it produces.","GM counter — principle: radiation ionises the gas in a tube and the ions are multiplied by a strong field. Construction: a metal cylinder (cathode) with a thin central wire (anode) at about 400–1000 V, filled with argon at low pressure plus a quenching gas, and a thin mica window. Working: each particle entering produces a few ions; the electrons accelerate to the wire and cause an avalanche of ionisation, giving a pulse that is counted.","Solid-state detector — principle: radiation creates electron–hole pairs in a semiconductor. Construction: a reverse-biased p–n junction (silicon or germanium) with a wide depletion layer. Working: radiation entering the depletion layer creates electron–hole pairs that are swept to the electrodes, giving a pulse proportional to the energy deposited.","Solid-state detectors are compact, fast and measure energies accurately; GM counters are simple and sensitive but cannot measure energy."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",46]],"s":["Medical diagnosis (tracers): ¹³¹I to study the thyroid, ⁹⁹ᵐTc for bone, heart and brain scans, ²⁴Na to detect blood-flow blockages; PET scans use positron emitters such as ¹⁸F.","Radiotherapy: ⁶⁰Co γ-rays and other sources destroy cancer cells; ¹³¹I treats thyroid cancer.","Sterilisation of surgical instruments and food preservation by γ-irradiation.","Biological research: ¹⁴C and ³²P tracers follow photosynthesis and the uptake of fertilisers by plants; radiation is used to produce improved crop varieties and to control insect pests (sterile insect technique).","Radiocarbon dating (¹⁴C) finds the age of biological remains."]},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",47]],"s":["Elementary particles are classified as hadrons and leptons (plus force carriers).","Hadrons feel the strong force and are made of quarks. Baryons (three quarks, half-integer spin) include protons (uud) and neutrons (udd); mesons (quark–antiquark pairs, integer spin) include pions and kaons.","Leptons do not feel the strong force and have no known internal structure: electron, muon, tau and their three neutrinos (plus antiparticles). They have spin ½.","Quarks are the fundamental constituents of hadrons. There are six flavours: up, down, strange, charm, bottom (beauty) and top (truth), with charges +2/3 e or −1/3 e. They carry colour charge and are never found free (quark confinement); they combine into colour-neutral hadrons.","Together with the force-carrying bosons (photon, gluons, W and Z), these form the Standard Model."]}],"numericals":[{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",49],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",50]],"s":["⁴He nucleus: 2 protons + 2 neutrons. Mass of nucleons = 2(1.007276) + 2(1.008665) = 4.031882 u.","Mass of ⁴He nucleus = 4.001506 u.","Mass defect Δm = 4.031882 − 4.001506 = 0.030376 u.","Binding energy = 0.030376 × 931.5 = 28.3 MeV."],"a":"Δm = 0.03038 u; E_b = 28.3 MeV"},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",51],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",52]],"s":["¹⁴N: 7 protons + 7 neutrons. Mass of nucleons = 7(1.007276) + 7(1.008665) = 14.111587 u.","Δm = 14.111587 − 13.999234 = 0.112353 u.","E_b = 0.112353 × 931.5 = 104.66 MeV."],"a":"104.66 MeV"},{"q":[["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",53],["phys20-exercise-mcqs-12-16-conceptual-comprehensive-numericals-380-381",54]],"s":["T½ = 1.6 × 10³ years = 1.6 × 10³ × 3.156 × 10⁷ s = 5.05 × 10¹⁰ s.","λ = 0.693/T½ = 0.693/5.05 × 10¹⁰","λ = 1.37 × 10⁻¹¹ s⁻¹ ≈ 1.4 × 10⁻¹¹ s⁻¹"],"a":"1.4 × 10⁻¹¹ s⁻¹","n":"The book writes radium as ²²⁶₈₆Ra; radium's atomic number is 88 (Z = 86 is radon)."},{"q":[["phys20-numericals-4-6-and-general-glossary-382-383",0],["phys20-numericals-4-6-and-general-glossary-382-383",1]],"s":["Number of nuclei in 1 kg: N = (1000/235) × 6.022 × 10²³ = 2.56 × 10²⁴.","Total energy = N × Q = 2.56 × 10²⁴ × 208 MeV","= 5.33 × 10²⁶ MeV (≈ 8.5 × 10¹³ J)"],"a":"≈ 5.32 × 10²⁶ MeV"},{"q":[["phys20-numericals-4-6-and-general-glossary-382-383",2],["phys20-numericals-4-6-and-general-glossary-382-383",3]],"s":["Reaction: ⁶Li + ²H → 2 ⁴He + 22.3 MeV (0.023940 u).","Mass balance: m(⁶Li) + m(²H) = 2m(⁴He) + 0.023940 u.","m(⁶Li) = 2(4.002603) + 0.023940 − 2.014102","m(⁶Li) = 6.015044 u"],"a":"6.01504 u","n":"The book prints the deuteron mass as 4.002603 u (the α mass repeated); the deuteron mass 2.014102 u gives the printed answer."},{"q":[["phys20-numericals-4-6-and-general-glossary-382-383",4],["phys20-numericals-4-6-and-general-glossary-382-383",5]],"s":["β⁻ decay: ²³⁴Th → ²³⁴Pa + e⁻ + ν̄. With atomic masses the electron masses cancel: Q = [m(Th) − m(Pa)] × 931.5 MeV.","With the printed masses: Δm = 234.0436 − 234.0428 = 0.0008 u, so Q = 0.0008 × 931.5 = 0.75 MeV.","With precise masses (²³⁴Th = 234.043601 u, ²³⁴Pa = 234.043308 u): Δm = 0.000293 u, Q = 0.27 MeV."],"a":"≈ 0.27 MeV (precise masses)","n":"The printed masses are rounded to four decimals, which gives 0.75 MeV; the printed answer 0.26967 MeV corresponds to the precise atomic masses."}]}}});
